// --- Lesson 1: Topographic Maps ---
blockNum = 0;
const ch1_lesson1 = [
  t(1, '## What Are Topographic Maps?\n\nA **topographic map** is a detailed two-dimensional representation of a portion of the Earth\'s surface. It shows both natural and human-made (constructed) features using symbols, colours, and contour lines.\n\nIn South Africa, the Chief Directorate: National Geo-spatial Information produces 1:50 000 topographic maps that cover the entire country.\n\n### Key Features of a 1:50 000 Topographic Map\n\n| Feature | Meaning |\n|---------|---------|\n| **Scale** | 1:50 000 means 1 cm on the map = 50 000 cm (500 m) in real life |\n| **Contour lines** | Brown lines joining places of equal height above sea level |\n| **Contour interval** | Usually 20 m on a 1:50 000 map — the vertical difference between consecutive contour lines |\n| **Grid references** | Used to locate features using the numbered grid squares |\n| **Conventional symbols** | Standard symbols for roads, railways, buildings, rivers, dams, etc. |\n| **Legend/key** | Explanation of all symbols used on the map |\n\n### Natural vs Constructed Features\n\n- **Natural features**: Rivers, hills, valleys, vegetation, coastlines\n- **Constructed features**: Roads, railways, bridges, dams, buildings, power lines, schools, churches'),
  q(2, 'On a 1:50 000 topographic map, what does the scale mean in practical terms?',
    ['1 cm on the map represents 500 m on the ground', '1 cm on the map represents 50 m on the ground', '1 cm on the map represents 5 km on the ground', '1 cm on the map represents 50 km on the ground'], 0,
    'The scale 1:50 000 means 1 unit on the map equals 50 000 of the same units in reality. So 1 cm on the map = 50 000 cm = 500 m = 0.5 km on the ground.'),
  t(3, '## Reading Map Symbols\n\nTopographic maps use standardised **conventional symbols** to represent features. You need to learn these for map work assessments.\n\n### Common Symbols to Know\n\n| Symbol Type | Examples |\n|-------------|----------|\n| **Roads** | National route (thick red/brown line), main road (thinner red line), secondary road (yellow), minor road (white) |\n| **Railways** | Black line with cross-hatches |\n| **Rivers** | Blue lines — thicker for larger rivers |\n| **Dams** | Blue shaded area with a dam wall symbol |\n| **Buildings** | Black/red squares or rectangles |\n| **Schools** | Black square with a flag symbol |\n| **Churches** | Black square with a cross |\n| **Spot heights** | A dot with a number showing the exact height in metres |\n| **Trigonometric beacon** | A triangle with a height value — a surveyed point of known elevation |\n| **Vegetation** | Green shading for plantations, orchards, and vineyards |\n\n### Compass Direction (Bearing)\n\nNorth is always at the **top** of a topographic map. The eight main compass directions are:\n- **N** (0°/360°), **NE** (45°), **E** (90°), **SE** (135°)\n- **S** (180°), **SW** (225°), **W** (270°), **NW** (315°)'),
  fb(4, 'On a topographic map, contour lines are coloured ___ and join places of equal height above sea level. The vertical difference between consecutive contour lines is called the contour ___.',
    ['brown', 'interval'],
    'Contour lines are drawn in brown on South African topographic maps. The contour interval on a 1:50 000 map is usually 20 m, meaning each successive contour line represents a change of 20 m in elevation.'),
  t(5, '## Grid References\n\nGrid references allow you to locate any feature on a topographic map precisely.\n\n### 4-Figure Grid Reference\n\nA 4-figure grid reference identifies the **grid square** in which a feature is located.\n\n**Method:**\n1. Read the number on the **horizontal** (bottom) grid line to the **left** of the feature — this is the **easting** (you go along the corridor first)\n2. Read the number on the **vertical** (side) grid line **below** the feature — this is the **northing** (then up the stairs)\n3. Combine: easting first, then northing\n\n*Example:* A feature in the square with easting 35 and northing 78 has a 4-figure grid reference of **3578**.\n\n### 6-Figure Grid Reference\n\nA 6-figure grid reference locates a feature more precisely **within** a grid square by dividing each square into tenths.\n\n**Method:**\n1. Find the easting (e.g., 35), then estimate tenths across the square (e.g., 7 tenths = 357)\n2. Find the northing (e.g., 78), then estimate tenths up the square (e.g., 4 tenths = 784)\n3. Combine: **357784**\n\n**Remember:** "Along the corridor, up the stairs" — eastings first, then northings.'),
  q(6, 'What is the correct method for reading a grid reference on a topographic map?',
    ['Read the easting (horizontal) first, then the northing (vertical) — "along the corridor, up the stairs"', 'Read the northing first, then the easting', 'Read the latitude first, then the longitude', 'Start from the top-right corner of the map'], 0,
    'The golden rule is "along the corridor, up the stairs." You first read the easting (the horizontal grid line number to the left of the feature), then the northing (the vertical grid line number below the feature).'),
  t(7, '## Contour Lines and Landforms\n\nContour lines tell us about the **shape of the land** (relief). By reading the pattern of contour lines, you can identify landforms.\n\n### Key Contour Patterns\n\n| Contour Pattern | Landform | Description |\n|-----------------|----------|-------------|\n| Lines close together | **Steep slope** | The closer the contour lines, the steeper the slope |\n| Lines far apart | **Gentle slope** | Widely spaced contours indicate a gradual slope |\n| Concentric circles (decreasing inward) | **Hill/Mountain** | Height increases toward the centre |\n| Concentric circles (increasing inward) | **Depression/Basin** | Height decreases toward the centre (tick marks point inward) |\n| V-shapes pointing uphill | **River valley** | Contour lines form a V pointing upstream |\n| V-shapes pointing downhill | **Spur/Ridge** | A ridge of land extending from a hill |\n| Evenly spaced, parallel lines | **Uniform slope** | A slope with consistent gradient |\n\n### Calculating Gradient\n\n**Gradient** measures how steep a slope is:\n\n**Formula:** Gradient = Vertical distance (difference in height) ÷ Horizontal distance\n\n*Example:* If two points are 100 m apart in height and 2 000 m apart horizontally:\n- Gradient = 100 ÷ 2 000 = 1/20\n- This means the land rises 1 m for every 20 m of horizontal distance\n- Written as **1:20**'),
  fb(8, 'When contour lines are close together, the slope is ___. The formula for gradient is vertical distance divided by ___ distance.',
    ['steep', 'horizontal'],
    'Closely spaced contour lines indicate a steep slope because there is a large change in height over a short horizontal distance. Gradient is calculated as the vertical height difference divided by the horizontal distance.'),
  q(9, 'On a topographic map, what landform is indicated when contour lines form V-shapes pointing uphill (towards higher ground)?',
    ['A river valley', 'A spur or ridge', 'A flat plateau', 'A depression'], 0,
    'When contour lines form V-shapes pointing towards higher ground, they indicate a river valley. The river flows in the base of the V, and the V points upstream. This is the opposite of a spur, where V-shapes point downhill.'),
  t(10, '## Scale and Distance\n\n### Measuring Distance on a Topographic Map\n\n**Straight-line distance:**\n1. Measure the distance between two points on the map in centimetres using a ruler\n2. Multiply by the scale factor: distance (cm) × 50 000 = actual distance (cm)\n3. Convert to metres or kilometres\n\n*Example:* 3.4 cm on a 1:50 000 map = 3.4 × 50 000 = 170 000 cm = 1 700 m = 1.7 km\n\n**Curved distance (e.g., along a river or road):**\n1. Place a piece of string along the curved feature on the map\n2. Straighten the string and measure its length in cm\n3. Apply the scale as above\n\n### Line Scale vs Ratio Scale\n\n- **Ratio scale**: Written as 1:50 000 (a ratio)\n- **Line scale**: A drawn bar on the map showing distances — useful because it still works if the map is enlarged or reduced\n- **Word scale**: Written in words, e.g., "1 cm represents 500 m"\n\nAll three express the same idea, but the **line scale** is most reliable because it changes size proportionally with the map.'),
  q(11, 'If a distance measures 6.2 cm on a 1:50 000 topographic map, what is the actual distance on the ground?',
    ['3.1 km', '31 km', '310 m', '0.31 km'], 0,
    '6.2 cm × 50 000 = 310 000 cm = 3 100 m = 3.1 km. Always remember to convert your final answer from centimetres to metres (÷ 100) or kilometres (÷ 100 000).'),
];

// --- Lesson 2: Orthophoto Maps and Aerial Photographs ---
blockNum = 0;
const ch1_lesson2 = [
  t(1, '## What Are Orthophoto Maps?\n\nAn **orthophoto map** is a photograph of the Earth\'s surface taken from an aircraft or satellite that has been **geometrically corrected** (orthorectified) so that the scale is uniform across the entire image. This means it can be used like a map — you can measure distances and directions accurately.\n\n### Key Features of a 1:10 000 Orthophoto Map\n\n| Feature | Detail |\n|---------|--------|\n| **Scale** | 1:10 000 means 1 cm = 100 m (much more detailed than topographic maps) |\n| **Appearance** | Looks like an aerial photograph with grid lines overlaid |\n| **No contour lines** | Relief is shown through shadows and shading, not contour lines |\n| **No conventional symbols** | Features appear as they look from above |\n| **Colour** | Uses natural colours — green vegetation, blue water, brown/grey buildings |\n| **Grid references** | Same grid system as topographic maps, allowing cross-referencing |\n\n### Comparing Topographic Maps and Orthophoto Maps\n\n| Feature | Topographic Map (1:50 000) | Orthophoto Map (1:10 000) |\n|---------|---------------------------|---------------------------|\n| Scale | Smaller scale, larger area | Larger scale, smaller area |\n| Symbols | Uses conventional symbols | Shows actual features |\n| Contour lines | Yes | No |\n| Height information | Through contours and spot heights | Limited (shadows only) |\n| Detail | Less detail | More detail |\n| Updates | Periodic updates | More current |'),
  q(2, 'What is the main advantage of an orthophoto map over a topographic map?',
    ['It shows much more detail because it uses a larger scale (1:10 000 vs 1:50 000)', 'It covers a larger area', 'It shows contour lines more clearly', 'It is always more up to date'], 0,
    'At a scale of 1:10 000, an orthophoto map shows five times more detail than a 1:50 000 topographic map. You can identify individual buildings, trees, and small features that would not appear on a topographic map.'),
  t(3, '## Identifying Features on Orthophoto Maps\n\nSince orthophoto maps do not use conventional symbols, you need to identify features by how they look from above.\n\n### Tips for Identifying Features\n\n| Feature | How It Appears |\n|---------|----------------|\n| **Buildings** | Regular rectangular shapes, usually grey or white |\n| **Roads** | Smooth grey/black lines of varying widths |\n| **Rivers** | Winding blue or dark lines with vegetation along banks |\n| **Dams** | Dark blue areas with straight edges (dam walls) |\n| **Cultivated fields** | Regular geometric patterns, often in different colours depending on crops |\n| **Natural vegetation** | Irregular green patches |\n| **Plantations** | Orderly rows of trees (darker green) |\n| **Sports fields** | Rectangular or oval green areas with markings |\n| **Informal settlements** | Dense clusters of small, irregular structures |\n| **Industries** | Large rectangular buildings with parking areas |\n\n### Shadows on Orthophoto Maps\n\nIn the Southern Hemisphere, the sun is in the **north**, so shadows fall **southward**. Shadows help you:\n- Determine which side is **north** (the sunlit side)\n- Estimate the **height** of buildings and trees\n- Identify valleys and ridges'),
  fb(4, 'On an orthophoto map, the scale is 1:10 000, which means 1 cm on the map equals ___ metres on the ground. In the Southern Hemisphere, shadows on aerial photographs fall towards the ___ because the sun is in the north.',
    ['100', 'south'],
    'At 1:10 000 scale, 1 cm = 10 000 cm = 100 m. Since South Africa is in the Southern Hemisphere, the sun appears in the northern sky, casting shadows to the south.'),
  t(5, '## Using Orthophoto Maps with Topographic Maps\n\nOrthophoto maps and topographic maps are designed to work **together**. They use the same grid system, so you can cross-reference features between them.\n\n### How to Cross-Reference\n\n1. Find a feature on the orthophoto map and note its grid reference\n2. Locate the same grid reference on the topographic map\n3. The topographic map will give you additional information like height (contour lines) and feature names\n4. The orthophoto map will show you what the feature actually looks like from above\n\n### Reading Height from an Orthophoto Map\n\nSince orthophoto maps do not have contour lines, you determine height by:\n- Cross-referencing with the topographic map\n- Looking at shadow lengths (longer shadows = taller features)\n- Reading spot heights if they are overlaid\n\n### Settlement Patterns on Orthophoto Maps\n\nYou can identify different settlement patterns:\n- **Linear settlement**: Houses along a road or river (stretched out in a line)\n- **Nucleated/clustered settlement**: Houses grouped around a central point (shop, church)\n- **Dispersed/scattered settlement**: Houses spread out, usually in farming areas\n- **Urban settlement**: Dense, regular grid patterns with many buildings'),
  q(6, 'How can you determine the height of features when using an orthophoto map that has no contour lines?',
    ['Cross-reference with the topographic map using the same grid reference system', 'Measure the size of buildings', 'Count the number of streets', 'Look at the colour of the features'], 0,
    'Since orthophoto maps and topographic maps use the same grid system, you can find any feature on the orthophoto map and then look at the corresponding area on the topographic map to read contour lines and spot heights.'),
  t(7, '## Vertical Aerial Photographs\n\nA **vertical aerial photograph** is taken from directly above the ground, looking straight down. It differs from an orthophoto map in that it has NOT been geometrically corrected.\n\n### Features of Aerial Photographs\n\n| Feature | Detail |\n|---------|--------|\n| **Scale** | Not uniform — larger near the centre, smaller at the edges |\n| **Distortion** | Objects at the edges may appear tilted or displaced |\n| **Relief displacement** | Tall objects lean away from the centre of the photograph |\n| **No grid lines** | Cannot use grid references directly |\n| **Overlap** | Photos overlap to create stereo pairs for 3D viewing |\n\n### Using Aerial Photos for Information Gathering\n\nAerial photographs are useful for:\n- **Land-use planning**: Seeing how land is used (farming, housing, industry)\n- **Environmental monitoring**: Tracking deforestation, erosion, or flooding\n- **Urban planning**: Identifying growth patterns and infrastructure needs\n- **Disaster assessment**: Viewing damage after floods, fires, or storms\n- **Historical comparison**: Comparing old and new photos to see how an area has changed'),
  fb(8, 'Unlike an orthophoto map, a vertical aerial photograph has NOT been geometrically ___. On an aerial photograph, tall objects lean away from the ___ of the image due to relief displacement.',
    ['corrected', 'centre'],
    'An orthophoto map has been corrected (orthorectified) to remove distortions, giving it a uniform scale. In an uncorrected aerial photograph, relief displacement causes tall objects to appear to lean outward from the centre.'),
  q(9, 'Which of the following is a key difference between an orthophoto map and a vertical aerial photograph?',
    ['An orthophoto map has been geometrically corrected to have a uniform scale', 'An orthophoto map shows more colour', 'A vertical aerial photograph is always taken from a satellite', 'A vertical aerial photograph always includes contour lines'], 0,
    'The key difference is geometric correction (orthorectification). An orthophoto map has been processed to remove distortions, making its scale uniform and allowing accurate measurements. A raw aerial photograph has distortion, especially at the edges.'),
  t(10, '## Map Skills in South African Context\n\nMap skills are essential for everyday life and many careers in South Africa:\n\n### Practical Applications\n\n- **Town planning**: Using maps to plan new housing developments, schools, and roads\n- **Agriculture**: Farmers use maps to identify soil types, water sources, and plan irrigation\n- **Mining**: Geological maps help locate mineral deposits — important for South Africa\'s mining industry\n- **Tourism**: Maps guide visitors to national parks like Kruger, Table Mountain, and the Drakensberg\n- **Emergency services**: Fire, ambulance, and rescue services use maps to navigate quickly\n- **Environmental management**: Tracking erosion, deforestation, and water quality\n\n### Modern Mapping Technology\n\n- **GPS (Global Positioning System)**: Uses satellites to determine exact position (latitude, longitude, altitude)\n- **GIS (Geographic Information Systems)**: Computer systems that store, analyse, and display geographic data in layers\n- **Google Earth/Maps**: Free tools that combine satellite imagery, aerial photography, and map data\n- **Drones**: Increasingly used for detailed aerial surveys, especially in agriculture and construction\n\nWhile digital tools are powerful, understanding how to read paper topographic and orthophoto maps remains a fundamental skill.'),
];

