// --- Lesson 1: The Rise of Nazi Germany ---
blockNum = 0;
const ch2_lesson1 = [
  t(1, '## The End of World War I and the Treaty of Versailles\n\nWorld War I (1914-1918) was one of the deadliest conflicts in history. It ended with Germany\'s defeat and the signing of the **Treaty of Versailles** in June 1919.\n\n### Key Terms of the Treaty of Versailles\n\n| Term | Detail |\n|------|--------|\n| **War guilt clause (Article 231)** | Germany had to accept full blame for causing the war |\n| **Reparations** | Germany had to pay £6.6 billion in compensation to the Allies |\n| **Territory** | Germany lost 13% of its land, including Alsace-Lorraine (to France), the Polish Corridor, and all overseas colonies |\n| **Military** | Army limited to 100 000 men; no tanks, air force, or submarines; the Rhineland was demilitarised |\n| **League of Nations** | A new international organisation to maintain peace — Germany was initially excluded |\n\n### German Reactions\n\nGermans were outraged by the treaty. They called it a **Diktat** (dictated peace) because Germany had no say in its terms. Many Germans:\n- Felt the treaty was unjust and humiliating\n- Blamed the politicians who signed it ("November criminals" or "stab in the back")\n- Suffered economically from reparations and territorial losses\n- Wanted revenge\n\nThe treaty created deep resentment in Germany that Adolf Hitler would later exploit.'),
  q(2, 'Why did many Germans call the Treaty of Versailles a "Diktat"?',
    ['Because Germany had no say in its terms — it was dictated to them by the Allies', 'Because it was written in a foreign language', 'Because it was a fair treaty that Germany agreed to', 'Because it was signed by a dictator'], 0,
    'A Diktat means a dictated or imposed settlement. Germany was not allowed to negotiate the treaty\'s terms — they had to accept or face invasion. This humiliation fuelled German resentment and nationalism throughout the 1920s and 1930s.'),
  t(3, '## The Weimar Republic and Its Problems\n\nAfter the Kaiser abdicated in November 1918, Germany became a republic with a democratic constitution drawn up in the city of **Weimar**.\n\n### Weaknesses of the Weimar Republic\n\n1. **Associated with defeat**: Many Germans blamed the republic for signing the Treaty of Versailles\n2. **Political instability**: Proportional representation led to many small parties and coalition governments that were weak and short-lived\n3. **Attacks from left and right**: Communists (Spartacist uprising, 1919) and right-wing nationalists (Kapp Putsch, 1920) both tried to overthrow the government\n4. **Hyperinflation (1923)**: Germany printed money to pay reparations, causing the currency to collapse — a loaf of bread cost billions of marks\n5. **Brief recovery (1924-1929)**: Under Gustav Stresemann, Germany recovered with American loans (Dawes Plan) and rejoined international relations\n\n### The Great Depression Hits Germany\n\nWhen the **Wall Street Crash** struck in October 1929:\n- American banks recalled their loans to Germany\n- German businesses collapsed and banks failed\n- By 1932, **6 million Germans** were unemployed (about 30% of the workforce)\n- People lost faith in democracy and turned to extreme parties\n- The **Nazi Party** and the **Communist Party** both gained massive support'),
  fb(4, 'In 1923, Germany experienced ___, when the government printed money to pay reparations and the currency lost almost all its value. By 1932, ___ million Germans were unemployed due to the Great Depression.',
    ['hyperinflation', '6'],
    'Hyperinflation in 1923 destroyed the savings of the German middle class, creating deep economic insecurity. The Great Depression from 1929 caused 6 million Germans to lose their jobs, pushing desperate voters towards the Nazi Party.'),
  t(5, '## Hitler and the Rise of the Nazi Party\n\n**Adolf Hitler** (1889-1945) was born in Austria and fought in World War I. He joined the small German Workers\' Party in 1919, which he renamed the **National Socialist German Workers\' Party (NSDAP or Nazi Party)**.\n\n### Key Nazi Beliefs\n\n1. **Racial superiority**: Germans ("Aryans") were the master race; Jews, Roma, and others were inferior\n2. **Anti-Semitism**: Jews were blamed for Germany\'s problems — a scapegoat\n3. **Lebensraum**: "Living space" — Germany needed to expand eastward\n4. **Anti-communism**: Hatred of Marxism and the Soviet Union\n5. **Führerprinzip**: The Führer (leader) had absolute authority — no democracy\n6. **Anti-Versailles**: Rejection of the Treaty of Versailles\n\n### How Hitler Came to Power\n\n| Date | Event |\n|------|-------|\n| **1923** | Beer Hall Putsch — failed attempt to seize power in Munich; Hitler imprisoned, wrote *Mein Kampf* |\n| **1928** | Nazi Party wins only 12 seats in the Reichstag (parliament) |\n| **1930** | After the Depression hits, Nazis win 107 seats |\n| **1932** | Nazis become the largest party with 230 seats; Hitler demands chancellorship |\n| **30 Jan 1933** | President Hindenburg appoints Hitler as **Chancellor** of Germany |\n| **Feb 1933** | Reichstag fire — Hitler blames communists and suspends civil liberties |\n| **Mar 1933** | **Enabling Act** — gives Hitler power to make laws without the Reichstag; democracy ends |'),
  q(6, 'What was the significance of the Enabling Act of March 1933?',
    ['It gave Hitler the power to make laws without the Reichstag, effectively ending democracy in Germany', 'It enabled Germany to rebuild its military', 'It enabled Jews to leave Germany', 'It enabled free elections to take place'], 0,
    'The Enabling Act allowed Hitler to bypass parliament and rule by decree for four years. This was the legal death of German democracy. Within months, all other political parties were banned and Germany became a one-party dictatorship.'),
  t(7, '## Life in Nazi Germany\n\nOnce in power, Hitler transformed Germany into a **totalitarian state** — the government controlled all aspects of life.\n\n### The Nazi Police State\n\n- **SS (Schutzstaffel)**: Hitler\'s elite bodyguard, led by Heinrich Himmler; ran the concentration camps\n- **Gestapo**: The secret police; spied on citizens, arrested opponents without trial\n- **Concentration camps**: Opponents were imprisoned without trial (Dachau, Sachsenhausen)\n- **Propaganda**: Joseph Goebbels controlled all media, films, radio, art, and books; book burnings destroyed "un-German" works\n- **Hitler Youth**: All boys aged 10-18 had to join; trained in Nazi ideology and military skills\n- **League of German Girls (BDM)**: Prepared girls for motherhood and supporting the state\n\n### Persecution of Jews (1933-1939)\n\n- 1933: Boycott of Jewish businesses; Jews dismissed from civil service\n- 1935: **Nuremberg Laws** stripped Jews of citizenship and banned marriage between Jews and "Aryans"\n- 1938: **Kristallnacht** ("Night of Broken Glass") — organised attacks on Jewish homes, businesses, and synagogues; 91 killed, 30 000 arrested\n- Jews were progressively excluded from schools, professions, public spaces, and economic life'),
  fb(8, 'The ___ Laws of 1935 stripped Jews of German citizenship. The Nazi propaganda minister who controlled all media was Joseph ___.',
    ['Nuremberg', 'Goebbels'],
    'The Nuremberg Laws were the legal foundation of Nazi anti-Semitism. Joseph Goebbels was the Minister of Propaganda, responsible for controlling all information and spreading Nazi ideology through radio, film, newspapers, and mass rallies.'),
  q(9, 'Why did many ordinary Germans support the Nazi Party in the early 1930s?',
    ['Unemployment was at 30% and people were desperate for strong leadership and economic recovery', 'Hitler promised to keep the Treaty of Versailles', 'Germany was experiencing an economic boom', 'The Weimar Republic was too stable and people wanted change'], 0,
    'With 6 million unemployed, many Germans turned to the Nazi Party out of desperation. Hitler promised jobs, national pride, and a strong Germany. The Nazis also used propaganda effectively and blamed Jews and communists for Germany\'s problems, giving people a simple answer to complex problems.'),
  t(10, '## The Reasons for Public Support of the Nazi Party\n\nThe Nazi Party gained support for specific reasons that we must understand historically:\n\n### Why Germans Supported Hitler\n\n1. **Economic recovery**: Hitler reduced unemployment through massive public works (autobahns, buildings) and rearmament; by 1939, unemployment was almost zero\n2. **National pride**: Hitler restored German pride by rejecting Versailles, rebuilding the military, and reclaiming lost territory\n3. **Fear of communism**: Many middle-class Germans and industrialists supported the Nazis as a barrier against communism\n4. **Effective propaganda**: The Nazis used modern media (radio, film, rallies) to spread their message\n5. **Scapegoating**: Blaming Jews and others provided simple answers to complex problems\n6. **Terror**: Those who opposed the regime risked arrest, imprisonment, or death — making open opposition dangerous\n\n### Lesson for South Africa\n\nThe rise of Nazi Germany shows how democracy can be destroyed when:\n- People are desperate and afraid\n- Leaders exploit hatred and prejudice\n- Citizens are willing to give up freedom for security\n- A minority group is scapegoated and dehumanised\n\nThis connects directly to South Africa\'s own experience with apartheid, where racial prejudice was used to maintain political power.'),
];

// --- Lesson 2: World War II in Europe and the Pacific ---
blockNum = 0;
const ch2_lesson2 = [
  t(1, '## The Outbreak of World War II\n\nAfter coming to power, Hitler systematically broke the Treaty of Versailles and expanded German territory:\n\n### Timeline of Aggression\n\n| Date | Event |\n|------|-------|\n| **1935** | Reintroduction of conscription; German military expansion |\n| **1936** | Remilitarisation of the Rhineland; Rome-Berlin Axis with Italy |\n| **1938** | **Anschluss**: Union with Austria (forbidden by Versailles) |\n| **1938** | **Munich Agreement**: Britain and France allowed Hitler to take the Sudetenland (part of Czechoslovakia) — appeasement |\n| **1939** | Hitler took the rest of Czechoslovakia |\n| **1939** | **Nazi-Soviet Pact**: Non-aggression agreement between Hitler and Stalin; secret plan to divide Poland |\n| **1 Sep 1939** | **Germany invaded Poland** |\n| **3 Sep 1939** | **Britain and France declared war on Germany** — World War II began |\n\n### What Was Appeasement?\n\n**Appeasement** was the policy of giving in to Hitler\'s demands to avoid war. British Prime Minister **Neville Chamberlain** famously returned from Munich claiming "peace for our time." Appeasement failed because it only encouraged Hitler to demand more.'),
  q(2, 'Why did the policy of appeasement fail to prevent World War II?',
    ['It encouraged Hitler to make more demands because he believed Britain and France would not fight', 'It was too aggressive towards Germany', 'Hitler was genuinely interested in peace', 'It successfully prevented the war for many years'], 0,
    'Each time Britain and France gave in to Hitler\'s demands, he took it as a sign of weakness and demanded more. The Munich Agreement (1938) gave him the Sudetenland, but within months he took the rest of Czechoslovakia. Appeasement proved that compromise with a dictator only delays conflict.'),
  t(3, '## World War II in Europe\n\n### Blitzkrieg and Early German Victories\n\nHitler\'s **Blitzkrieg** ("lightning war") strategy used fast-moving tanks, aircraft, and infantry to overwhelm opponents:\n\n- **1939**: Poland defeated in weeks; divided between Germany and the USSR\n- **1940**: Denmark, Norway, Netherlands, Belgium, and **France** all fell to Germany; France surrendered in June 1940\n- **1940**: **Battle of Britain** — Germany tried to bomb Britain into submission; the RAF defended successfully, forcing Hitler to cancel his invasion plans\n- **1941**: Germany invaded Yugoslavia and Greece\n\n### Operation Barbarossa (June 1941)\n\nOn 22 June 1941, Hitler launched the invasion of the Soviet Union — the largest military operation in history:\n- 3 million German soldiers attacked along a 2 900 km front\n- Initial success: the Germans advanced deep into Soviet territory\n- **Battle of Stalingrad (1942-1943)**: The turning point — the German 6th Army was surrounded and destroyed; 91 000 Germans surrendered\n- After Stalingrad, the Soviets slowly pushed the Germans back westward\n\n### The Holocaust\n\nDuring the war, the Nazis carried out the systematic murder of **six million Jews** and millions of others (Roma, disabled people, political opponents) in **extermination camps** such as Auschwitz, Treblinka, and Sobibor. This genocide is known as the **Holocaust**.'),
  fb(4, 'Hitler\'s military strategy of using fast-moving tanks and aircraft was called ___ ("lightning war"). The turning point of the war on the Eastern Front was the Battle of ___ (1942-1943).',
    ['Blitzkrieg', 'Stalingrad'],
    'Blitzkrieg combined air power, tanks, and motorised infantry to overwhelm opponents quickly before they could organise defences. The Battle of Stalingrad was the deadliest battle in history, and Germany\'s defeat there marked the beginning of the end for Hitler.'),
  t(5, '## The End of World War II in Europe\n\n### D-Day and the Liberation of Europe\n\nOn **6 June 1944 (D-Day)**, Allied forces (mainly British, American, and Canadian) landed on the beaches of **Normandy**, France — the largest seaborne invasion in history.\n\n- Over 150 000 troops landed on the first day\n- Despite heavy casualties, the Allies established a beachhead and began liberating France\n- Paris was liberated in August 1944\n- The Allies advanced eastward while the Soviets advanced westward\n\n### The Fall of Germany\n\n- By early 1945, Germany was being attacked from both sides\n- Soviet forces reached **Berlin** in April 1945\n- **30 April 1945**: Hitler committed suicide in his bunker in Berlin\n- **8 May 1945**: Germany surrendered unconditionally — **VE Day** (Victory in Europe Day)\n\n### The Cost of World War II in Europe\n\n| Country | Estimated Deaths |\n|---------|------------------|\n| **Soviet Union** | 27 million (the greatest loss) |\n| **Germany** | 7-9 million |\n| **Poland** | 6 million (including 3 million Jews) |\n| **Yugoslavia** | 1 million |\n| **France** | 600 000 |\n| **United Kingdom** | 450 000 |\n| **Total worldwide** | 70-85 million |'),
  q(6, 'Which country suffered the greatest number of deaths during World War II?',
    ['The Soviet Union, with approximately 27 million dead', 'Germany, with approximately 9 million dead', 'The United Kingdom, with approximately 450 000 dead', 'The United States, with approximately 400 000 dead'], 0,
    'The Soviet Union bore the heaviest cost of World War II, with an estimated 27 million deaths — both military and civilian. This enormous sacrifice is central to understanding post-war Soviet attitudes and the Cold War that followed.'),
  t(7, '## World War II in the Pacific\n\n### Japanese Expansion\n\nJapan, allied with Germany and Italy (the **Axis Powers**), sought to build a vast empire in Asia and the Pacific:\n\n- Japan invaded **Manchuria** (China) in 1931 and launched a full-scale invasion of China in 1937\n- **7 December 1941**: Japan attacked the US naval base at **Pearl Harbor**, Hawaii — bringing the USA into the war\n- Japan quickly conquered Southeast Asia: the Philippines, Malaya, Singapore, Burma, the Dutch East Indies (Indonesia)\n\n### America in the War\n\n- The attack on Pearl Harbor united America behind the war effort\n- The US fought a brutal **island-hopping campaign** across the Pacific, taking islands like Iwo Jima and Okinawa\n- Japanese soldiers fought with extreme determination and rarely surrendered\n- **Kamikaze** pilots deliberately crashed their planes into Allied ships\n\n### The Atomic Bombs\n\n- **6 August 1945**: The US dropped an atomic bomb ("Little Boy") on **Hiroshima**, killing approximately 80 000 people instantly (over 140 000 by the end of 1945)\n- **9 August 1945**: A second atomic bomb ("Fat Man") was dropped on **Nagasaki**, killing approximately 40 000 instantly\n- **15 August 1945**: Japan surrendered — **VJ Day** (Victory over Japan Day)\n\nThe decision to use atomic bombs remains one of the most debated events in history. The US argued it saved lives by avoiding a land invasion of Japan. Critics say it was unnecessary and targeted civilians.'),
  fb(8, 'On 7 December 1941, Japan attacked the US naval base at ___, bringing America into the war. The first atomic bomb was dropped on the Japanese city of ___ on 6 August 1945.',
    ['Pearl Harbor', 'Hiroshima'],
    'The surprise attack on Pearl Harbor destroyed much of the US Pacific fleet and killed over 2 400 Americans. It immediately brought the USA into World War II. The atomic bombing of Hiroshima introduced the nuclear age and remains deeply controversial.'),
  q(9, 'Why is the use of atomic bombs on Hiroshima and Nagasaki historically controversial?',
    ['Because they killed mostly civilians and some argue the bombs were unnecessary to end the war', 'Because they failed to end the war', 'Because Japan had already surrendered before the bombs were dropped', 'Because the bombs were dropped on military bases only'], 0,
    'The atomic bombs killed over 200 000 people, the vast majority civilians. Some historians argue Japan was already close to surrender, making the bombs unnecessary. Others argue that without the bombs, a land invasion would have cost even more lives. This debate continues today.'),
  t(10, '## South Africa and World War II\n\nSouth Africa played a significant role in World War II, but the war also deepened divisions within white South African society.\n\n### South Africa\'s Participation\n\n- Prime Minister **Jan Smuts** took South Africa into the war on the Allied side in September 1939\n- Approximately **330 000 South Africans** served in the war (of all races)\n- South African forces fought in **North Africa** (against Rommel), **East Africa**, **Italy**, and **Madagascar**\n- **Black, Coloured, and Indian South Africans** served but were not allowed to carry weapons in combat roles — they served as drivers, labourers, and stretcher-bearers\n- Over **11 000 South Africans** died during the war\n\n### Division Among White South Africans\n\n- Many **Afrikaners** opposed the war and sympathised with Germany:\n  - The **Ossewabrandwag** (OB) was a pro-Nazi organisation that carried out sabotage in South Africa\n  - **B.J. Vorster** (later Prime Minister) and other future apartheid leaders were interned for pro-Nazi activities\n- The war deepened the divide between English-speaking whites (who supported the war) and many Afrikaners (who opposed it)\n- The **National Party** under D.F. Malan opposed the war and used anti-war sentiment to build support for its 1948 election victory\n\nIronically, just three years after the defeat of Nazi racism, South Africa would begin implementing its own system of racial domination — **apartheid**.'),
];

