// --- Lesson 1: Measuring and Understanding Development ---
blockNum = 0;
const ch3_lesson1 = [
  t(1, '## What Is Development?\n\n**Development** refers to the process of improving the quality of life of a country\'s people. It involves not only economic growth but also social, political, and environmental improvements.\n\n### Dimensions of Development\n\n| Dimension | What It Includes |\n|-----------|------------------|\n| **Economic** | Income levels, employment, industrial growth, trade |\n| **Social** | Education, healthcare, housing, clean water, sanitation |\n| **Political** | Democracy, human rights, good governance, rule of law |\n| **Environmental** | Clean air and water, sustainable resource use, biodiversity |\n\n### Developed vs Developing Countries\n\n| Feature | Developed Countries | Developing Countries |\n|---------|--------------------|-----------------------|\n| **Income** | High GDP per capita | Low to middle GDP per capita |\n| **Life expectancy** | 75-85 years | 50-70 years |\n| **Literacy** | Over 95% | Varies widely (50-90%) |\n| **Urbanisation** | Highly urbanised | Mix of rural and urban |\n| **Infrastructure** | Well-developed roads, hospitals, schools | Often lacking or uneven |\n| **Examples** | Japan, Germany, Australia | South Africa, Nigeria, India |\n\nSouth Africa is classified as a **developing country** but has features of both — modern cities like Johannesburg exist alongside deep rural poverty in provinces like the Eastern Cape and Limpopo.'),
  q(2, 'Why is South Africa classified as a developing country despite having modern cities?',
    ['Because it has extreme inequality — modern cities exist alongside deep rural poverty', 'Because it has no industry', 'Because it has a small population', 'Because it is located in Africa'], 0,
    'South Africa has one of the highest levels of inequality in the world (Gini coefficient of about 0.63). While cities like Johannesburg and Cape Town are modern, millions of South Africans live in poverty without adequate housing, water, or sanitation.'),
  t(3, '## Measuring Development: The Human Development Index (HDI)\n\nThe **Human Development Index (HDI)** was created by the United Nations Development Programme (UNDP) to measure development more broadly than just income.\n\n### Components of the HDI\n\nThe HDI combines three indicators:\n\n1. **Life expectancy at birth** — measures health and longevity\n2. **Education** — measured by mean years of schooling and expected years of schooling\n3. **Gross National Income (GNI) per capita** — measures standard of living (adjusted for purchasing power)\n\n### HDI Scale\n\n| HDI Range | Category | Examples |\n|-----------|----------|----------|\n| 0.800-1.000 | Very high | Norway (0.957), Germany, Australia |\n| 0.700-0.799 | High | Brazil, China, South Africa (0.713) |\n| 0.550-0.699 | Medium | India, Kenya, Ghana |\n| Below 0.550 | Low | Niger, Chad, Central African Republic |\n\n### Why HDI Is Better Than GDP Alone\n\n- **GDP per capita** only measures average income — it does not show how income is distributed\n- A country can have high GDP but poor health and education (e.g., some oil-rich nations)\n- The HDI gives a more complete picture by including health and education\n- However, the HDI still does not measure inequality, safety, or environmental sustainability'),
  fb(4, 'The Human Development Index (HDI) combines three indicators: life expectancy, ___, and Gross National Income per capita. South Africa\'s HDI places it in the ___ human development category.',
    ['education', 'high'],
    'The HDI measures development across health (life expectancy), knowledge (education), and standard of living (GNI per capita). South Africa\'s HDI of about 0.713 places it in the "high" category, but this average hides massive inequality between rich and poor.'),
  t(5, '## Factors Affecting Development\n\nWhy are some countries more developed than others? The reasons are complex and interconnected.\n\n### Historical Factors\n\n1. **Colonialism**: European colonisation exploited resources and labour in Africa, Asia, and the Americas; colonial borders often divided communities and created artificial states\n2. **Slavery and the slave trade**: Millions of Africans were enslaved, devastating societies and economies for centuries\n3. **Apartheid** (in South Africa): Decades of racial discrimination created massive inequalities that persist today\n\n### Economic Factors\n\n1. **Trade imbalances**: Developing countries often export raw materials (cheap) and import manufactured goods (expensive)\n2. **Debt**: Many developing countries owe large debts to international banks and institutions\n3. **Unfair trade rules**: International trade agreements often favour wealthy nations\n\n### Social Factors\n\n1. **Health**: Diseases like HIV/AIDS, malaria, and tuberculosis reduce the workforce and place strain on healthcare systems\n2. **Education**: Low literacy and limited access to education reduce economic productivity\n3. **Gender inequality**: When women are denied education and economic opportunities, the whole society suffers\n\n### Political Factors\n\n1. **Corruption**: Diverts resources away from development\n2. **Conflict and war**: Destroys infrastructure and displaces populations\n3. **Poor governance**: Weak institutions cannot deliver services effectively'),
  q(6, 'How did colonialism contribute to underdevelopment in Africa?',
    ['Colonial powers exploited resources and labour, created artificial borders, and left behind weak institutions', 'Colonialism brought only benefits to Africa', 'Africa was already developed before colonialism', 'Colonialism had no lasting effects on Africa'], 0,
    'Colonialism extracted Africa\'s wealth (minerals, labour, crops) for the benefit of European powers. It also disrupted existing societies, created artificial borders that divided communities, imposed foreign systems of government, and left behind economies dependent on exporting raw materials.'),
  t(7, '## Development in South Africa\n\n### South Africa\'s Development Challenges\n\nSouth Africa faces unique development challenges rooted in its history of colonialism and apartheid:\n\n| Challenge | Detail |\n|-----------|--------|\n| **Inequality** | Among the highest in the world; Gini coefficient ≈ 0.63 |\n| **Unemployment** | Over 30% (over 60% among youth) |\n| **Poverty** | Over 55% of the population lives below the poverty line |\n| **HIV/AIDS** | One of the highest infection rates in the world; life expectancy affected |\n| **Education** | Quality is highly unequal; many rural schools lack basic resources |\n| **Housing** | Millions live in informal settlements without adequate services |\n| **Service delivery** | Many communities lack clean water, electricity, and sanitation |\n\n### Government Development Programmes\n\n- **RDP (Reconstruction and Development Programme, 1994)**: Focused on meeting basic needs — housing, water, electricity, healthcare\n- **GEAR (Growth, Employment and Redistribution, 1996)**: Market-friendly economic policy to attract investment\n- **NDP (National Development Plan, 2012)**: Vision 2030 — aims to eliminate poverty and reduce inequality\n- **Social grants**: Over 18 million South Africans receive government grants (child support, old age pension, disability)\n- **Free basic services**: Free water (6 kl/month) and electricity (50 kWh/month) for poor households'),
  fb(8, 'South Africa\'s Gini coefficient of approximately ___ indicates it is one of the most unequal countries in the world. The ___ (National Development Plan) is the government\'s Vision 2030 to eliminate poverty.',
    ['0.63', 'NDP'],
    'The Gini coefficient measures inequality on a scale of 0 (perfect equality) to 1 (perfect inequality). South Africa\'s score of about 0.63 reflects extreme inequality — a legacy of apartheid. The NDP (2012) sets goals for reducing poverty and inequality by 2030.'),
  q(9, 'Which of the following is NOT a component of the Human Development Index?',
    ['Military spending', 'Life expectancy at birth', 'Education (years of schooling)', 'Gross National Income per capita'], 0,
    'The HDI measures health (life expectancy), education (schooling), and standard of living (GNI per capita). Military spending is not included. The HDI is designed to measure human well-being, not military power.'),
  t(10, '## Sustainable Development and Alternative Approaches\n\nTraditional development has focused on economic growth, but this has often come at the expense of the environment and social equality.\n\n### What Is Sustainable Development?\n\n**Sustainable development** means meeting the needs of the present without compromising the ability of future generations to meet their own needs.\n\n### The Three Pillars of Sustainable Development\n\n1. **Economic sustainability**: Growth that creates jobs and reduces poverty without depleting resources\n2. **Social sustainability**: Development that is inclusive and reduces inequality\n3. **Environmental sustainability**: Protecting natural resources, reducing pollution, and addressing climate change\n\n### Alternative Development Approaches\n\n- **Community-based development**: Local communities identify their own needs and lead their own development projects\n- **Fair trade**: Ensuring producers in developing countries receive fair prices for their goods\n- **Microfinance**: Small loans to poor people to start businesses (e.g., Grameen Bank in Bangladesh)\n- **Education for girls**: One of the most effective development strategies — educated women have fewer children, earn more, and invest in their families\n\n### South African Examples\n\n- **Working for Water programme**: Clears invasive alien plants while creating jobs\n- **Expanded Public Works Programme (EPWP)**: Government creates temporary jobs in infrastructure, environment, and social services\n- **Community food gardens**: Local projects to address food security'),
];

// --- Lesson 2: Opportunities for Development ---
blockNum = 0;
const ch3_lesson2 = [
  t(1, '## Opportunities for Development\n\nDespite challenges, developing countries — including South Africa — have real opportunities to improve the lives of their people.\n\n### Health and Welfare\n\nImproving health is one of the most effective ways to promote development:\n\n- **Immunisation programmes**: Preventing diseases like measles, polio, and tuberculosis saves lives and reduces healthcare costs\n- **Clean water and sanitation**: Providing clean drinking water and proper sanitation prevents waterborne diseases (cholera, typhoid, dysentery)\n- **HIV/AIDS treatment**: South Africa now has the world\'s largest antiretroviral (ARV) programme, significantly increasing life expectancy\n- **Primary healthcare clinics**: Government clinics provide free basic healthcare to millions of South Africans\n\n### Education\n\nEducation is the foundation of development:\n\n- **Universal primary education**: South Africa has achieved near-universal school enrolment, though quality varies enormously\n- **Skills development**: TVET colleges and learnership programmes train young people for employment\n- **Adult literacy programmes**: Helping adults who missed out on education during apartheid\n- **Technology in education**: Tablets, internet connectivity, and digital content are expanding access to quality learning materials\n\n### The Role of Technology\n\n- **Mobile phones**: South Africa has one of Africa\'s highest mobile phone penetration rates; mobile banking (like FNB\'s eWallet) reaches people without bank accounts\n- **Renewable energy**: South Africa is investing in solar and wind power to address energy shortages and reduce carbon emissions\n- **E-commerce**: Online platforms create new economic opportunities'),
  q(2, 'Why is education considered the foundation of development?',
    ['Educated people earn more, are healthier, and can participate more fully in democratic society', 'Education is only important for wealthy countries', 'Education has no effect on economic growth', 'Only university education matters for development'], 0,
    'Education improves individual earnings, health outcomes, and civic participation. Studies show that each additional year of schooling increases a person\'s income by approximately 10%. Educated women have fewer children and invest more in their families, creating a virtuous cycle of development.'),
  t(3, '## Political Stability and Good Governance\n\nDevelopment requires stable, accountable government:\n\n### Why Good Governance Matters\n\n1. **Rule of law**: Clear, fair laws protect people\'s rights and create a stable environment for business\n2. **Accountability**: Leaders must be answerable to the people through free and fair elections\n3. **Transparency**: Government finances and decisions should be open to public scrutiny\n4. **Fighting corruption**: Corruption diverts resources from schools, hospitals, and infrastructure\n5. **Independent judiciary**: Courts must be free from political interference to protect rights\n\n### South Africa\'s Democratic Institutions\n\n- **The Constitution (1996)**: One of the most progressive in the world; includes the Bill of Rights\n- **Independent courts**: The Constitutional Court can strike down laws that violate the Constitution\n- **Chapter 9 institutions**: The Public Protector, Human Rights Commission, Auditor-General, and others hold government accountable\n- **Free media**: Independent newspapers, radio, and television expose corruption and inform the public\n- **Civil society organisations**: NGOs, community organisations, and trade unions advocate for people\'s rights\n\nHowever, South Africa faces ongoing challenges with corruption, state capture, and service delivery failures that undermine development.'),
  fb(4, 'South Africa\'s ___ (1996) is one of the most progressive in the world and includes the Bill of Rights. Institutions like the Public Protector and Human Rights Commission are called Chapter ___ institutions.',
    ['Constitution', '9'],
    'The South African Constitution, adopted in 1996, is the supreme law of the country. Chapter 9 institutions are independent bodies created by the Constitution to strengthen democracy and protect human rights.'),
  t(5, '## Economic Sustainability\n\n### South Africa\'s Economic Opportunities\n\nSouth Africa has significant economic resources and opportunities:\n\n| Sector | Opportunity |\n|--------|-------------|\n| **Mining** | World\'s largest reserves of platinum, manganese, and chromium; significant gold and diamond production |\n| **Agriculture** | Diverse climates support farming from subtropical fruits to grain crops; export-oriented wine, citrus, and deciduous fruit industries |\n| **Tourism** | Natural beauty (Table Mountain, Kruger Park, Garden Route), cultural heritage (Robben Island), and wildlife attract millions of visitors |\n| **Manufacturing** | Automotive industry (BMW, Mercedes-Benz, Toyota manufacture in SA); food processing |\n| **Financial services** | Sophisticated banking sector; Johannesburg Stock Exchange is Africa\'s largest |\n| **Technology** | Growing tech sector; South Africa is a hub for African fintech innovation |\n\n### Challenges to Economic Growth\n\n- **Load shedding**: Unreliable electricity supply damages economic productivity\n- **Skills shortage**: Mismatch between education and job market needs\n- **Brain drain**: Skilled workers emigrating to other countries\n- **Infrastructure backlogs**: Roads, rail, and ports need investment\n- **Red tape**: Complex regulations can discourage investment and entrepreneurship'),
  q(6, 'Which of the following is a major challenge to South Africa\'s economic development?',
    ['Unreliable electricity supply (load shedding) damages productivity', 'South Africa has no natural resources', 'South Africa has too few people', 'South Africa has no access to the sea'], 0,
    'Load shedding — scheduled power cuts due to insufficient electricity generation — has been one of South Africa\'s biggest economic challenges. It disrupts businesses, reduces productivity, and discourages investment. Eskom, the state power utility, has struggled to maintain ageing power stations.'),
  t(7, '## Environmental Sustainability\n\nDevelopment must not come at the cost of environmental destruction.\n\n### Environmental Challenges in South Africa\n\n1. **Water scarcity**: South Africa is a water-scarce country; demand is growing faster than supply\n2. **Pollution**: Air pollution from industry and mining; water pollution from acid mine drainage and sewage\n3. **Deforestation and land degradation**: Clearing of natural vegetation for farming and housing\n4. **Loss of biodiversity**: South Africa is one of the world\'s most biodiverse countries, but species are threatened by habitat loss\n5. **Climate change**: Rising temperatures, changing rainfall patterns, and more frequent droughts and floods\n\n### Solutions and Opportunities\n\n- **Renewable energy**: South Africa\'s Renewable Energy Independent Power Producer Procurement Programme (REIPPPP) has attracted billions in investment for wind and solar projects\n- **Water conservation**: Programmes like "Water Wise" encourage efficient water use; rainwater harvesting and greywater recycling\n- **Waste management**: Recycling programmes create jobs while reducing landfill waste\n- **Conservation**: National parks and marine protected areas preserve biodiversity; eco-tourism generates income\n- **Green economy**: The shift to a low-carbon economy creates new jobs in renewable energy, energy efficiency, and sustainable agriculture'),
  fb(8, 'South Africa is classified as a water-___ country, meaning demand is growing faster than supply. The shift to renewable energy and environmental protection is called the ___ economy.',
    ['scarce', 'green'],
    'South Africa receives below the world average rainfall, and water resources are unevenly distributed. The green economy refers to economic development that reduces environmental risks and ecological scarcities, creating sustainable growth.'),
  q(9, 'What is sustainable development?',
    ['Meeting the needs of the present without compromising the ability of future generations to meet their own needs', 'Development that focuses only on economic growth', 'Development that ignores environmental concerns', 'Development that benefits only wealthy countries'], 0,
    'Sustainable development balances economic growth, social equity, and environmental protection. It was defined by the Brundtland Commission in 1987 and has become the guiding principle for development worldwide.'),
  t(10, '## International Cooperation for Development\n\n### Key International Organisations\n\n| Organisation | Role |\n|--------------|------|\n| **United Nations (UN)** | Coordinates international development efforts; Sustainable Development Goals (SDGs) |\n| **World Bank** | Provides loans and grants to developing countries for development projects |\n| **International Monetary Fund (IMF)** | Provides financial assistance to countries in economic difficulty |\n| **African Union (AU)** | Promotes political and economic integration across Africa |\n| **BRICS** | South Africa, Brazil, Russia, India, China cooperate on development issues |\n\n### The Sustainable Development Goals (SDGs)\n\nIn 2015, the UN adopted **17 Sustainable Development Goals** to be achieved by 2030:\n- No poverty (Goal 1)\n- Zero hunger (Goal 2)\n- Good health and well-being (Goal 3)\n- Quality education (Goal 4)\n- Gender equality (Goal 5)\n- Clean water and sanitation (Goal 6)\n- Affordable and clean energy (Goal 7)\n- And 10 more covering industry, inequality, cities, climate, oceans, land, peace, and partnerships\n\nSouth Africa has committed to achieving these goals, but progress has been uneven — poverty, inequality, and unemployment remain the country\'s greatest challenges.'),
];

// =============================================================================
// CHAPTER 4 (History – Term 2): The Cold War
// =============================================================================

// --- Lesson 1: Origins and Conflicts of the Cold War ---
blockNum = 0;
const ch4_lesson1 = [
  t(1, '## The Beginning of the Cold War\n\nThe **Cold War** (1947-1991) was a period of tension and rivalry between the **United States (USA)** and the **Soviet Union (USSR)** that dominated international relations for over four decades.\n\n### What Was the Cold War?\n\n- It was called "cold" because the two superpowers never fought each other **directly** in a full-scale war\n- Instead, they competed through:\n  - **Proxy wars**: Wars fought in other countries (Korea, Vietnam, Angola)\n  - **Arms race**: Building up nuclear weapons\n  - **Space race**: Competing to explore space\n  - **Propaganda**: Each side tried to prove its system was superior\n  - **Espionage**: Spying on each other\n\n### The Superpowers Compared\n\n| Feature | USA | USSR |\n|---------|-----|------|\n| **Political system** | Democracy (elected president, Congress) | One-party communist state |\n| **Economic system** | Capitalism (private ownership, free market) | Communism (state ownership, central planning) |\n| **Values** | Individual freedom, private enterprise | Collective welfare, state control |\n| **Alliance** | NATO (North Atlantic Treaty Organisation, 1949) | Warsaw Pact (1955) |\n| **Nuclear weapons** | First atomic bomb: 1945 | First atomic bomb: 1949 |'),
  q(2, 'Why was the Cold War called "cold"?',
    ['Because the USA and USSR never fought each other directly in a full-scale war', 'Because it took place in cold climates', 'Because both sides used cold weapons (swords, spears)', 'Because it was a short conflict'], 0,
    'The Cold War was "cold" because despite extreme tension, the USA and USSR avoided direct military conflict with each other. Instead, they competed through proxy wars, the arms race, espionage, and propaganda. The threat of nuclear war made direct conflict too dangerous.'),
  t(3, '## Origins of the Cold War\n\n### Why Did the Cold War Begin?\n\nDuring World War II, the USA and USSR had been allies against Nazi Germany. But once the common enemy was defeated, deep ideological differences surfaced:\n\n1. **Ideological conflict**: Capitalism vs communism — two fundamentally opposed systems\n2. **Soviet expansion in Eastern Europe**: After WWII, the USSR established communist governments in Poland, Czechoslovakia, Hungary, Romania, Bulgaria, and East Germany — creating a "buffer zone" against future invasion\n3. **The Iron Curtain**: In 1946, Winston Churchill declared that "an iron curtain has descended across the continent" — dividing Europe into communist East and capitalist West\n4. **The Truman Doctrine (1947)**: US President Harry Truman promised to support any country resisting communist takeover — the policy of "containment"\n5. **The Marshall Plan (1947)**: The USA provided $13 billion in economic aid to rebuild Western Europe and prevent the spread of communism\n6. **The Berlin Blockade (1948-1949)**: The USSR blocked all road, rail, and canal access to West Berlin; the West responded with a massive **airlift** that supplied the city for 11 months\n\n### The Division of Germany\n\nAfter WWII, Germany was divided into four zones (American, British, French, Soviet). In 1949:\n- The three Western zones became **West Germany** (Federal Republic of Germany) — democratic and capitalist\n- The Soviet zone became **East Germany** (German Democratic Republic) — communist\n- **Berlin**, located inside East Germany, was also divided into West and East Berlin'),
  fb(4, 'Winston Churchill used the term "Iron ___" to describe the division of Europe into communist East and capitalist West. The ___ Doctrine of 1947 committed the USA to supporting countries resisting communism.',
    ['Curtain', 'Truman'],
    'Churchill\'s "Iron Curtain" speech (1946) described how Soviet-dominated communist states had cut off Eastern Europe from the West. The Truman Doctrine committed the USA to the policy of "containment" — preventing the spread of communism.'),
  t(5, '## The Arms Race and the Nuclear Age\n\nThe Cold War was defined by the threat of **nuclear war**:\n\n### Timeline of the Nuclear Arms Race\n\n| Date | Event |\n|------|-------|\n| **1945** | USA drops atomic bombs on Hiroshima and Nagasaki |\n| **1949** | USSR tests its first atomic bomb — ends US nuclear monopoly |\n| **1952** | USA tests the first hydrogen bomb (H-bomb) — 1 000 times more powerful than Hiroshima |\n| **1953** | USSR tests its first hydrogen bomb |\n| **1957** | USSR launches **Sputnik**, the first satellite — begins the space race |\n| **1961** | USSR tests the **Tsar Bomba** — largest nuclear weapon ever detonated (50 megatons) |\n| **1962** | **Cuban Missile Crisis** — the closest the world came to nuclear war |\n\n### Mutually Assured Destruction (MAD)\n\nBy the 1960s, both superpowers had enough nuclear weapons to destroy each other completely. This concept was called **Mutually Assured Destruction (MAD)**:\n- If one side launched nuclear weapons, the other would retaliate\n- Both sides would be completely destroyed\n- Therefore, neither side would dare to start a nuclear war\n- MAD was terrifying but it arguably prevented a direct superpower war\n\nAt the peak, the USA had about **31 000 nuclear weapons** and the USSR had about **40 000**.'),
  q(6, 'What was the concept of Mutually Assured Destruction (MAD)?',
    ['The idea that both superpowers had enough nuclear weapons to destroy each other, making nuclear war suicidal for both sides', 'A plan to use nuclear weapons only against small countries', 'A strategy to build defensive shields against nuclear attack', 'A peace agreement between the USA and USSR'], 0,
    'MAD was the grim logic of the nuclear age: both sides had such destructive capability that any nuclear attack would lead to total mutual annihilation. Paradoxically, this terrifying situation helped prevent nuclear war because neither side could "win" a nuclear conflict.'),
  t(7, '## The Cuban Missile Crisis (October 1962)\n\nThe **Cuban Missile Crisis** was the most dangerous moment of the Cold War — the world came the closest it has ever been to nuclear war.\n\n### Background\n\n- In 1959, **Fidel Castro** led a revolution in Cuba, establishing a communist government just 150 km from the USA\n- The USA attempted to overthrow Castro at the **Bay of Pigs** (1961) — a humiliating failure\n- The USSR, led by **Nikita Khrushchev**, decided to place nuclear missiles in Cuba\n\n### The Crisis (14-28 October 1962)\n\n1. **14 October**: US spy planes discovered Soviet missile sites under construction in Cuba\n2. **22 October**: President **John F. Kennedy** announced a naval **blockade** (quarantine) of Cuba\n3. **24 October**: Soviet ships carrying missiles approached the blockade line — the world held its breath\n4. The Soviet ships stopped and turned back\n5. **28 October**: Khrushchev agreed to remove the missiles in exchange for:\n   - A US promise not to invade Cuba\n   - Secret removal of US missiles from Turkey\n\n### Significance\n\n- The crisis showed how easily the Cold War could turn into nuclear war\n- Both sides were shaken; a **hotline** (direct communication link) was established between Washington and Moscow\n- Led to the **Nuclear Test Ban Treaty (1963)** — banning nuclear tests in the atmosphere'),
  fb(8, 'During the Cuban Missile Crisis, President ___ imposed a naval blockade on Cuba. Soviet leader ___ agreed to remove the missiles, narrowly avoiding nuclear war.',
    ['Kennedy', 'Khrushchev'],
    'President John F. Kennedy chose a naval blockade rather than a military strike, giving diplomacy time to work. Khrushchev agreed to withdraw the missiles, though the USA secretly agreed to remove its own missiles from Turkey. It was the closest the world came to nuclear war.'),
  q(9, 'What was the main outcome of the Cuban Missile Crisis?',
    ['Both sides realised how dangerous the nuclear standoff was, leading to improved communication and arms control agreements', 'The USA successfully invaded Cuba', 'The Soviet Union won control of the Western Hemisphere', 'Both sides agreed to destroy all their nuclear weapons'], 0,
    'The Cuban Missile Crisis was a wake-up call for both superpowers. It led to the establishment of a hotline between Washington and Moscow, the Nuclear Test Ban Treaty (1963), and eventually the Non-Proliferation Treaty (1968). Both sides began to pursue arms control.'),
  t(10, '## The Space Race\n\nThe USA and USSR competed to demonstrate technological superiority through space exploration:\n\n### Key Events\n\n| Date | Event | Country |\n|------|-------|---------|\n| **1957** | **Sputnik** — first artificial satellite | USSR |\n| **1957** | **Laika** — first animal in orbit (a dog) | USSR |\n| **1961** | **Yuri Gagarin** — first human in space | USSR |\n| **1961** | **Alan Shepard** — first American in space | USA |\n| **1962** | **John Glenn** — first American to orbit Earth | USA |\n| **1969** | **Apollo 11** — **Neil Armstrong** becomes first human to walk on the Moon | USA |\n| **1975** | **Apollo-Soyuz** — first joint US-Soviet space mission | USA & USSR |\n\n### Significance of the Space Race\n\n- Both sides used space achievements as **propaganda** to prove the superiority of their system\n- The space race drove technological innovation — computers, satellite communications, weather forecasting\n- Sputnik shocked America and led to massive investment in science education\n- The Moon landing in 1969 was seen as an American victory in the space race\n- The Apollo-Soyuz mission (1975) showed that cooperation was possible even during the Cold War'),
];

// --- Lesson 2: The Division of Germany and the End of the Cold War ---
blockNum = 0;
const ch4_lesson2 = [
  t(1, '## The Division of Germany and the Berlin Wall\n\n### Why Was Germany Divided?\n\nAfter World War II, the Allied powers (USA, Britain, France, USSR) divided Germany into four occupation zones. Berlin, deep inside the Soviet zone, was also divided.\n\n- **West Germany (FRG)**: Democratic, capitalist, rebuilt with Marshall Plan aid; became prosperous\n- **East Germany (GDR)**: Communist, state-controlled economy; lower standard of living\n\n### The Berlin Wall (1961)\n\nBy 1961, the difference between East and West was stark. Millions of East Germans had fled to the West through Berlin — a massive embarrassment for the communist regime.\n\nOn **13 August 1961**, the East German government built a **wall** through Berlin overnight:\n- Families were separated\n- Armed guards, barbed wire, watchtowers, and a "death strip" prevented escape\n- At least **140 people** were killed trying to cross the wall\n- The Berlin Wall became the most powerful symbol of the Cold War division of Europe\n\n### What the Wall Represented\n\n- The failure of communism to compete with capitalism in providing a good quality of life\n- The need for authoritarian regimes to use force to prevent their citizens from leaving\n- The division of the world into two hostile blocs'),
  q(2, 'Why was the Berlin Wall built in 1961?',
    ['To stop East Germans from fleeing to West Berlin, which was a massive embarrassment to the communist regime', 'To protect West Berlin from invasion', 'To separate different ethnic groups', 'To create a tourist attraction'], 0,
    'By 1961, approximately 3.5 million East Germans had fled to the West through Berlin. This brain drain of skilled workers and professionals was devastating to the East German economy and a propaganda disaster for communism. The wall was built to trap people, not protect them.'),
  t(3, '## Areas of Conflict: The Cold War Around the World\n\nThe Cold War was fought through **proxy wars** — conflicts in other countries where the superpowers supported opposing sides:\n\n### The Korean War (1950-1953)\n\n- Korea was divided at the **38th parallel** after WWII: communist North (supported by USSR/China) and democratic South (supported by USA)\n- In 1950, North Korea invaded the South\n- The UN (led by the USA) intervened; China entered the war on North Korea\'s side\n- The war ended in 1953 with Korea still divided along the 38th parallel — where it remains today\n\n### The Vietnam War (1955-1975)\n\n- Vietnam was divided into communist North (Ho Chi Minh) and non-communist South\n- The USA sent troops to prevent South Vietnam from falling to communism (the "domino theory")\n- Over **58 000 American soldiers** and **2-3 million Vietnamese** died\n- The USA withdrew in 1973; Vietnam was reunified under communist rule in 1975\n- The Vietnam War showed the limits of American military power\n\n### Angola and Southern Africa\n\n- After Portuguese colonial rule ended in 1975, Angola was plunged into civil war\n- The **MPLA** (supported by the USSR and Cuba) fought against **UNITA** (supported by the USA and apartheid South Africa)\n- **South Africa** invaded Angola in 1975 to support UNITA and fight the ANC\'s ally, the MPLA\n- The **Battle of Cuito Cuanavale (1988)** was a turning point — South African forces were fought to a standstill by Angolan and Cuban troops\n- This contributed to Namibian independence (1990) and the end of apartheid'),
  fb(4, 'The ___ War (1950-1953) divided Korea at the 38th parallel and ended without a peace treaty. In southern Africa, the Battle of Cuito ___ (1988) was a turning point that helped end apartheid.',
    ['Korean', 'Cuanavale'],
    'The Korean War is sometimes called the "forgotten war." Korea remains divided today. The Battle of Cuito Cuanavale in Angola was significant because it demonstrated the limits of South African military power and contributed to negotiations that led to Namibian independence and the end of apartheid.'),
  t(5, '## The End of the Cold War\n\nThe Cold War ended between 1989 and 1991 through a remarkable series of events.\n\n### Mikhail Gorbachev and Reform\n\nIn 1985, **Mikhail Gorbachev** became leader of the USSR. He introduced two key policies:\n\n1. **Glasnost** ("openness"): Greater freedom of speech, press, and political debate\n2. **Perestroika** ("restructuring"): Economic reforms to make the Soviet economy more efficient\n\nGorbachev also:\n- Reduced Soviet military spending and forces\n- Allowed Eastern European countries to choose their own path\n- Improved relations with the USA and signed arms reduction treaties\n\n### The Fall of the Berlin Wall (9 November 1989)\n\n- Throughout 1989, democratic movements swept across Eastern Europe:\n  - **Poland**: Solidarity trade union won free elections\n  - **Hungary**: Opened its border with Austria, allowing East Germans to escape West\n  - **Czechoslovakia**: The "Velvet Revolution" peacefully overthrew the communist government\n- On **9 November 1989**, the East German government announced that citizens could cross the border freely\n- Thousands of Berliners climbed the wall and began tearing it down in scenes of joyful celebration\n- Germany was officially **reunified** on **3 October 1990**'),
  q(6, 'What were Mikhail Gorbachev\'s two key reform policies?',
    ['Glasnost (openness in political debate) and perestroika (restructuring of the economy)', 'Blitzkrieg (lightning war) and Lebensraum (living space)', 'Containment and the Truman Doctrine', 'Appeasement and isolationism'], 0,
    'Glasnost allowed greater freedom of expression and political discussion, while perestroika aimed to reform the inefficient Soviet economy. Ironically, these reforms intended to save communism actually helped bring about its collapse, as people used their new freedoms to demand even more change.'),
  t(7, '## The Collapse of the Soviet Union (1991)\n\n### Why Did the USSR Collapse?\n\n1. **Economic failure**: The Soviet planned economy could not compete with Western capitalism; shortages of basic goods were common\n2. **Military overstretch**: The costly war in Afghanistan (1979-1989) drained Soviet resources (the "Soviet Vietnam")\n3. **Gorbachev\'s reforms**: Glasnost and perestroika unintentionally weakened communist control\n4. **Nationalism**: The USSR was made up of 15 republics with different ethnic groups; they began demanding independence\n5. **Loss of Eastern Europe**: The fall of communist governments in Eastern Europe in 1989 removed the USSR\'s buffer zone\n6. **Failed coup (August 1991)**: Hard-line communists tried to overthrow Gorbachev but were defeated by democratic forces led by **Boris Yeltsin** (president of the Russian Republic)\n\n### The End\n\n- On **25 December 1991**, Gorbachev resigned as President of the USSR\n- The **Soviet Union officially dissolved** on **26 December 1991**\n- 15 independent republics emerged, with **Russia** as the largest\n- The Cold War was over; the USA was the world\'s only superpower\n\n### Impact on South Africa\n\nThe end of the Cold War had a direct impact on South Africa:\n- The apartheid government could no longer use the "communist threat" to justify its policies\n- Western countries, no longer needing South Africa as an anti-communist ally, increased pressure for democratic change\n- The ANC\'s alliance with the South African Communist Party was no longer seen as a threat\n- These changes contributed to the unbanning of the ANC and the release of Nelson Mandela in **February 1990**'),
  fb(8, 'The Soviet Union officially dissolved on 26 December ___. The end of the Cold War contributed to the release of Nelson ___ in February 1990.',
    ['1991', 'Mandela'],
    'The dissolution of the USSR on 26 December 1991 ended the Cold War. For South Africa, the end of Cold War tensions removed the apartheid government\'s excuse that it was fighting communism, and reduced Western support for the regime, accelerating the transition to democracy.'),
  q(9, 'How did the end of the Cold War affect the transition to democracy in South Africa?',
    ['The apartheid government could no longer use anti-communism to justify its policies, and Western countries increased pressure for change', 'It had no effect on South Africa', 'It caused South Africa to become communist', 'It led to a military invasion of South Africa'], 0,
    'During the Cold War, Western nations tolerated apartheid because South Africa was seen as a bulwark against communism in southern Africa. Once the Cold War ended, this strategic justification disappeared, and international pressure for democracy intensified.'),
  t(10, '## Lessons of the Cold War\n\nThe Cold War shaped the modern world in profound ways:\n\n### Key Lessons\n\n1. **Ideology can divide the world**: The conflict between capitalism and communism created a global power struggle that affected every continent\n2. **Nuclear weapons changed warfare**: The threat of mutual destruction prevented direct conflict between the superpowers but created the constant danger of annihilation\n3. **Proxy wars devastated developing countries**: Millions died in Korea, Vietnam, Angola, Mozambique, and elsewhere as superpowers fought through local conflicts\n4. **Propaganda shapes perceptions**: Both sides used media and propaganda to demonise the other\n5. **Change is possible**: The Cold War ended not through war but through internal reform (Gorbachev), people power (Eastern Europe), and economic reality\n\n### Relevance Today\n\n- The world is no longer bipolar (two superpowers) but **multipolar** (USA, China, Russia, EU, India)\n- Nuclear weapons still exist and remain a threat\n- Ideological competition continues in different forms\n- Many developing countries are still dealing with the consequences of Cold War-era proxy wars\n- South Africa\'s transition from apartheid to democracy was partly shaped by the end of the Cold War'),
];

// =============================================================================
// CHAPTER 5 (Geography – Term 3): Surface Forces That Shape the Earth
// =============================================================================

// --- Lesson 1: Weathering ---
blockNum = 0;
const ch5_lesson1 = [
  t(1, '## What Is Weathering?\n\n**Weathering** is the breaking down and decay of rocks **in situ** (in their original position) by the forces of weather and biological activity. Weathering does NOT involve the movement of rock material — that is **erosion**.\n\n### The Difference Between Weathering, Erosion, and Deposition\n\n| Process | Definition |\n|---------|------------|\n| **Weathering** | The breakdown of rock in its original position |\n| **Erosion** | The removal and transport of weathered material by water, wind, ice, or gravity |\n| **Deposition** | The laying down of transported material in a new location |\n\nThese three processes work together to shape the Earth\'s surface. Weathering weakens rock, erosion carries it away, and deposition builds up new landforms.\n\n### Types of Weathering\n\nThere are three main types:\n1. **Physical (mechanical) weathering**: Rock is broken into smaller pieces without changing its chemical composition\n2. **Chemical weathering**: Rock is changed into new substances through chemical reactions\n3. **Biological weathering**: Rock is broken down by the actions of living organisms'),
  q(2, 'What is the key difference between weathering and erosion?',
    ['Weathering breaks down rock in its original position, while erosion involves transport of the material', 'Weathering only affects soft rocks, while erosion affects hard rocks', 'Weathering is caused by wind and erosion is caused by water', 'There is no difference between weathering and erosion'], 0,
    'Weathering breaks rock down in situ (where it is). Erosion then removes and transports the weathered material. A boulder cracking due to temperature changes is weathering; a river carrying sand downstream is erosion.'),
  t(3, '## Physical (Mechanical) Weathering\n\nPhysical weathering breaks rock into smaller fragments without changing its chemical composition. The main types are:\n\n### 1. Block Disintegration (Thermal Expansion)\n\n- Occurs in areas with large **temperature differences** between day and night (e.g., the Karoo, Kalahari)\n- During the day, rocks heat up and expand; at night, they cool and contract\n- Different minerals expand at different rates, creating stress\n- Over time, the rock cracks along joints and breaks into angular blocks\n- Common in South Africa\'s hot, dry interior\n\n### 2. Exfoliation (Onion-skin Weathering)\n\n- Also caused by repeated heating and cooling\n- The outer layers of rock expand and contract more than the inner layers\n- Thin curved sheets of rock peel off like layers of an onion\n- Creates rounded rock surfaces (e.g., the dome-shaped inselbergs in the Matopos Hills)\n\n### 3. Frost Weathering (Freeze-Thaw)\n\n- Water seeps into cracks in rock and **freezes** at night\n- When water freezes, it expands by about 9%, exerting enormous pressure on the rock\n- Repeated freezing and thawing widens cracks until pieces break off\n- Important in the **Drakensberg** mountains and other high-altitude areas of South Africa'),
  fb(4, 'Block disintegration occurs in areas with large ___ differences between day and night. Frost weathering is important in the ___ mountains of South Africa.',
    ['temperature', 'Drakensberg'],
    'Block disintegration is common in arid regions like the Karoo where daytime temperatures can be very high and nights very cold. Frost weathering requires temperatures that drop below freezing, which occurs regularly in the Drakensberg mountains (up to 3 482 m above sea level).'),
  t(5, '## Chemical Weathering\n\nChemical weathering changes the chemical composition of rock, creating new, weaker minerals. It is most effective in **warm, wet climates** (like KwaZulu-Natal and Mpumalanga).\n\n### Types of Chemical Weathering\n\n### 1. Carbonation\n\n- Rainwater absorbs carbon dioxide (CO₂) from the air, forming weak **carbonic acid**\n- This acid dissolves **limestone** and **dolomite** — rocks made of calcium carbonate\n- Creates features like sinkholes, caves, and underground passages\n- In South Africa: the **Cango Caves** (near Oudtshoorn) were formed by carbonation\n\n### 2. Oxidation\n\n- Oxygen in water reacts with iron-rich minerals in rock\n- This creates iron oxide (rust), which weakens the rock\n- Gives rock a reddish-brown colour\n- Common in South Africa\'s iron-rich soils and rocks\n\n### 3. Hydrolysis\n\n- Water reacts with minerals in rock (especially feldspar in granite)\n- The minerals are changed into soft **clay minerals**\n- The rock becomes weakened and crumbly\n- This is why granite landscapes often have a thick layer of decomposed rock (regolith)'),
  q(6, 'Which type of chemical weathering created the Cango Caves near Oudtshoorn?',
    ['Carbonation — carbonic acid dissolved the limestone rock', 'Oxidation — rust weakened the rock', 'Hydrolysis — water reacted with granite', 'Frost weathering — ice cracked the rock'], 0,
    'Carbonation occurs when rainwater (containing dissolved CO₂) forms carbonic acid, which slowly dissolves limestone and dolomite. Over millions of years, this creates underground caves and passages like the spectacular Cango Caves.'),
  t(7, '## Biological Weathering\n\nLiving organisms contribute to the breakdown of rock:\n\n### Types of Biological Weathering\n\n1. **Root wedging**: Tree and plant roots grow into cracks in rock, widening them as the roots grow thicker. Over time, this can split large boulders.\n\n2. **Burrowing animals**: Moles, earthworms, termites, and other burrowing animals mix and loosen soil and rock, exposing new surfaces to weathering.\n\n3. **Lichen and moss**: These organisms grow on rock surfaces and produce weak acids that dissolve minerals. They also trap moisture, promoting chemical weathering.\n\n4. **Human activity**: Mining, construction, and farming expose rock surfaces to weathering. In South Africa, mining operations (especially open-cast mining) dramatically increase the rate of weathering and erosion.\n\n### Factors That Affect the Rate of Weathering\n\n| Factor | Effect |\n|--------|--------|\n| **Climate** | Warm, wet climates accelerate chemical weathering; large temperature ranges promote physical weathering |\n| **Rock type** | Soft rocks (limestone, sandstone) weather faster than hard rocks (granite, quartzite) |\n| **Rock structure** | Jointed or fractured rock weathers faster because water can penetrate |\n| **Vegetation** | Plants protect rock from direct weather but roots can cause physical weathering |\n| **Time** | Weathering is a slow process — significant effects take thousands or millions of years |\n| **Aspect** | In the Southern Hemisphere, north-facing slopes receive more sun and weather differently |'),
  fb(8, 'Tree roots growing into cracks and splitting rock is called root ___. Lichen and moss contribute to biological weathering by producing weak ___ that dissolve minerals.',
    ['wedging', 'acids'],
    'Root wedging is a powerful form of biological weathering — growing roots can exert enough pressure to split boulders. Lichen produce organic acids (like oxalic acid) that slowly dissolve the mineral surface of rocks, contributing to both physical and chemical weathering.'),
  q(9, 'In which climate type is chemical weathering most effective?',
    ['Warm, wet climates like KwaZulu-Natal', 'Cold, dry climates like Antarctica', 'Hot, dry climates like the Sahara Desert', 'Cold, wet climates like Scandinavia'], 0,
    'Chemical weathering requires water and warmth. Water is needed as a solvent and transport medium for chemical reactions, and warmth speeds up these reactions. In South Africa, the warm, humid coastal regions (like KwaZulu-Natal) experience more chemical weathering than the dry interior.'),
  t(10, '## Weathering in the South African Landscape\n\nSouth Africa\'s diverse climates and rock types produce a variety of weathering landscapes:\n\n### Examples Across South Africa\n\n| Region | Dominant Weathering | Result |\n|--------|--------------------|---------|\n| **Karoo** | Physical (block disintegration) | Flat-topped koppies (hills) with rocky rubble at base |\n| **Drakensberg** | Frost weathering + chemical | Dramatic basalt cliffs and sandstone formations |\n| **KwaZulu-Natal coast** | Chemical (hydrolysis, oxidation) | Deep red/orange soils from decomposed rock |\n| **Cango Caves** | Chemical (carbonation) | Spectacular limestone caves with stalactites and stalagmites |\n| **Cape Fold Mountains** | Physical + chemical | Sandstone and shale formations |\n| **Mpumalanga Lowveld** | Chemical (warm, wet) + biological | Thick soil layer supporting dense vegetation |\n\n### Why Understanding Weathering Matters\n\n- **Agriculture**: Weathered rock produces soil — the type of soil depends on the parent rock and climate\n- **Construction**: Engineers must understand weathering when building roads, dams, and buildings\n- **Mining**: Weathering affects the stability of mine workings\n- **Tourism**: Weathering creates spectacular landscapes that attract visitors\n- **Environmental management**: Understanding weathering helps predict erosion risks and plan land use'),
];

// --- Lesson 2: Erosion and Deposition ---
blockNum = 0;
const ch5_lesson2 = [
  t(1, '## Rivers: Agents of Erosion and Deposition\n\nRivers are the most important agents of erosion and deposition in shaping South Africa\'s landscape.\n\n### How Rivers Erode\n\nRivers erode in four main ways:\n\n| Process | Description |\n|---------|-------------|\n| **Hydraulic action** | The force of moving water breaks rock and loosens material from the river banks and bed |\n| **Abrasion (corrasion)** | Rocks and pebbles carried by the river scrape against the bed and banks like sandpaper |\n| **Attrition** | Rocks carried by the river knock against each other, becoming smaller and rounder |\n| **Solution (corrosion)** | Acids in the water dissolve soluble rocks (like limestone) |\n\n### The Course of a River\n\nA river\'s characteristics change from source to mouth:\n\n| Feature | Upper Course | Middle Course | Lower Course |\n|---------|-------------|---------------|---------------|\n| **Gradient** | Steep | Moderate | Gentle |\n| **Valley shape** | Narrow, V-shaped | Wider, gentler V | Wide, flat floodplain |\n| **Main process** | Erosion (downcutting) | Erosion and deposition | Deposition |\n| **Channel** | Narrow, rocky | Wider, deeper | Wide, slow-moving |\n| **Features** | Waterfalls, rapids, gorges | Meanders, oxbow lakes | Floodplains, levees, deltas |'),
  q(2, 'Which erosion process involves rocks carried by a river scraping against the bed and banks?',
    ['Abrasion (corrasion)', 'Hydraulic action', 'Attrition', 'Solution'], 0,
    'Abrasion (also called corrasion) is like sandpaper — rocks and pebbles carried by the river grind against the bed and banks, wearing them away. It is most effective during floods when the river carries large amounts of sediment.'),
  t(3, '## River Features: Upper Course\n\n### Waterfalls\n\n**How waterfalls form:**\n1. A river flows over a band of **hard rock** lying on top of **softer rock**\n2. The soft rock is eroded more quickly by hydraulic action and abrasion\n3. The hard rock is left overhanging (an overhang develops)\n4. The overhang eventually collapses under its own weight\n5. The waterfall retreats upstream, leaving a steep-sided **gorge**\n6. At the base, the falling water creates a deep **plunge pool** through erosion\n\n### South African Example\n\nThe **Augrabies Falls** on the Orange River in the Northern Cape is one of the largest waterfalls in the world, with a drop of 56 metres. The falls have retreated upstream over millions of years, carving a deep gorge.\n\n### Gorges and Canyons\n\n- **Gorges** are steep-sided, narrow valleys cut by rivers through hard rock\n- Formed by the retreat of waterfalls or by rivers downcutting through elevated terrain\n- South African examples: the **Blyde River Canyon** (Mpumalanga) — one of the largest green canyons in the world'),
  fb(4, 'A waterfall forms where a river flows over ___ rock lying on top of softer rock. The falling water creates a deep ___ pool at the base through erosion.',
    ['hard', 'plunge'],
    'Waterfalls form due to differential erosion — soft rock is worn away faster than hard rock, creating a step in the river profile. The turbulent falling water erodes a deep plunge pool at the base. Over time, the waterfall retreats upstream, forming a gorge.'),
  t(5, '## River Features: Middle and Lower Course\n\n### Meanders\n\nIn the middle and lower course, the river begins to wind from side to side in curves called **meanders**:\n\n**How meanders form:**\n1. Water flows faster on the **outside** of a bend (more erosion) and slower on the **inside** (more deposition)\n2. The outside bank is eroded, forming a steep **river cliff** (cut bank)\n3. Material is deposited on the inside, forming a gentle **slip-off slope** (point bar)\n4. Over time, the meander grows larger and more exaggerated\n\n### Oxbow Lakes\n\n**How oxbow lakes form:**\n1. A meander loop becomes very tight — the neck of the meander narrows\n2. During a flood, the river breaks through the narrow neck, taking a straighter, shorter path\n3. The old meander loop is cut off from the river and forms a crescent-shaped **oxbow lake**\n4. Over time, the oxbow lake fills with sediment and vegetation\n\n### Floodplains and Levees\n\n- A **floodplain** is the flat area on either side of a river in the lower course, formed by repeated flooding and deposition\n- **Levees** are raised banks along the river channel, formed when floods deposit the heaviest sediment closest to the river\n- Floodplains have rich, fertile soil — ideal for farming (e.g., the floodplains of the Vaal and Orange Rivers)'),
  q(6, 'On a river meander, where does erosion occur and where does deposition occur?',
    ['Erosion occurs on the outside of the bend (forming a river cliff) and deposition occurs on the inside (forming a slip-off slope)', 'Erosion occurs on the inside and deposition on the outside', 'Both erosion and deposition occur equally on both sides', 'Neither erosion nor deposition occurs in meanders'], 0,
    'Water flows faster on the outside of a meander bend, causing erosion and forming a steep river cliff (cut bank). On the inside, the water is slower, so sediment is deposited, forming a gentle slip-off slope (point bar). This differential process causes meanders to migrate across the floodplain.'),
  t(7, '## Deposition Features\n\n### Levees and Deltas\n\n**Levees** form when a river floods and deposits its sediment load:\n- The heaviest material is deposited closest to the river channel\n- Over repeated floods, these deposits build up into raised banks (levees) along the river\n- The floodplain beyond the levees receives finer sediment (silt and clay)\n\n**Deltas** form where a river enters a calm body of water (lake, sea, ocean):\n- The river\'s velocity decreases and it can no longer carry its sediment load\n- Sediment is deposited in a fan shape at the river mouth\n- The river splits into distributaries\n- South African rivers rarely form large deltas because the coastline is steep and ocean currents are strong\n\n### The Impact of People on Soil Erosion\n\nHuman activities significantly increase erosion in South Africa:\n\n| Activity | Effect |\n|----------|--------|\n| **Agriculture** | Ploughing exposes soil; overgrazing removes vegetation cover |\n| **Construction** | Removing vegetation and disturbing soil during building |\n| **Mining** | Open-cast mining exposes vast areas of bare rock and soil |\n| **Deforestation** | Removing trees eliminates root systems that hold soil together |\n| **Informal settlements** | Often built on steep slopes without drainage, leading to erosion and mudslides |'),
  fb(8, 'A delta forms where a river enters a calm body of water and deposits its ___. Raised banks formed along rivers by repeated flooding are called ___.',
    ['sediment', 'levees'],
    'When a river reaches a lake or ocean, its speed drops and it can no longer carry its load. The sediment is deposited, forming a delta. Levees are natural embankments built up by repeated floods depositing coarse material close to the channel.'),
  q(9, 'Why do South African rivers rarely form large deltas?',
    ['Because the coastline is steep and strong ocean currents carry away deposited sediment', 'Because South African rivers carry no sediment', 'Because South Africa has no rivers reaching the ocean', 'Because the government prevents delta formation'], 0,
    'South Africa\'s coastline is relatively steep with strong ocean currents (the Agulhas Current on the east coast and the Benguela Current on the west coast) that carry sediment away before large deltas can form. This contrasts with rivers like the Nile or Mississippi that have extensive deltas.'),
  t(10, '## Erosion as a Problem in South Africa\n\nSouth Africa loses an estimated **300-400 million tonnes** of topsoil per year — a serious threat to agriculture and food security.\n\n### Types of Soil Erosion\n\n1. **Sheet erosion**: A thin layer of topsoil is washed away evenly by surface runoff — often not noticed until significant damage is done\n2. **Rill erosion**: Small channels (rills) form on slopes as water concentrates into narrow paths\n3. **Gully erosion (dongas)**: Deep channels cut into the landscape — extremely difficult to repair. In South Africa, these are called **dongas**\n\n### Preventing Erosion\n\n| Method | How It Works |\n|--------|--------------|\n| **Contour ploughing** | Ploughing along the contour (not downslope) slows water flow |\n| **Terracing** | Building steps into hillsides to slow water and trap soil |\n| **Cover crops** | Planting vegetation to protect bare soil between crop seasons |\n| **Windbreaks** | Rows of trees reduce wind erosion on farms |\n| **Gabions** | Wire baskets filled with rocks placed in dongas to slow water and trap soil |\n| **Maintaining vegetation** | Keeping natural vegetation along rivers (riparian zones) prevents bank erosion |\n\nErosion prevention is essential for South Africa\'s food security and water quality. Every South African depends on healthy soils and clean water.'),
];

