// CHAPTER 6 (History – Term 3): Turning Points in SA History Since 1948
// =============================================================================

// --- Lesson 1: Apartheid Laws and Resistance (1948-1960) ---
blockNum = 0;
const ch6_lesson1 = [
  t(1, '## The Creation of the Apartheid State (1948)\n\nIn 1948, the **National Party (NP)** under **D.F. Malan** won the general election and began implementing **apartheid** — a system of racial separation and white supremacy.\n\n### Key Apartheid Laws\n\n| Law | Year | What It Did |\n|-----|------|-------------|\n| **Population Registration Act** | 1950 | Classified every South African by race: White, Coloured, Indian, or Native (Black) |\n| **Group Areas Act** | 1950 | Assigned separate residential areas for each race; forced removals of people living in "wrong" areas |\n| **Separate Amenities Act** | 1953 | Separate (and unequal) public facilities: beaches, buses, benches, hospitals, toilets, etc. |\n| **Bantu Education Act** | 1953 | Created a separate, inferior education system for black South Africans |\n| **Pass Laws** | Expanded | Black people had to carry a "pass book" (dompas) at all times; controlled movement into white areas |\n| **Prohibition of Mixed Marriages Act** | 1949 | Banned marriage between white people and people of other races |\n| **Immorality Amendment Act** | 1950 | Banned sexual relations between white people and people of other races |\n| **Suppression of Communism Act** | 1950 | Banned the Communist Party and defined "communism" so broadly that any opposition could be labelled communist |\n\n### Hendrik Verwoerd and "Grand Apartheid"\n\n**Hendrik Verwoerd** (Minister of Native Affairs 1950-1958, Prime Minister 1958-1966) was the "architect of apartheid." He introduced **Bantu Education** and the **Bantustan** system — creating separate "homelands" for different ethnic groups, stripping black South Africans of their citizenship in South Africa.'),
  q(2, 'What was the purpose of the Population Registration Act of 1950?',
    ['To classify every South African by race, which then determined all their rights and opportunities', 'To register people to vote in democratic elections', 'To count the population for census purposes', 'To register people for military service'], 0,
    'The Population Registration Act was the foundation of apartheid — it classified every South African as White, Coloured, Indian, or Native (Black). This racial classification determined where you could live, work, go to school, who you could marry, and which public facilities you could use.'),
  t(3, '## The Defiance Campaign (1952)\n\nThe **Defiance Campaign** was the first large-scale, multi-racial campaign of civil disobedience against apartheid.\n\n### Organisation\n\n- Led by the **ANC** and the **South African Indian Congress (SAIC)**\n- Inspired by the non-violent resistance of **Mahatma Gandhi** (who had first practised passive resistance in South Africa)\n- Volunteers deliberately broke apartheid laws and courted arrest\n\n### What Happened\n\n- Volunteers entered "whites only" areas, used "whites only" facilities, and refused to carry passes\n- Over **8 500 people were arrested** — they accepted imprisonment as a badge of honour\n- The campaign spread across the country — Port Elizabeth, Johannesburg, Cape Town, Durban\n- **Nelson Mandela** was appointed volunteer-in-chief\n\n### Results\n\n- ANC membership grew from 7 000 to over **100 000**\n- The campaign showed that mass non-violent action was possible\n- However, the government responded with harsher laws and arrested leaders\n- The **Criminal Law Amendment Act (1953)** made it a criminal offence to break a law as a form of protest\n\nThe Defiance Campaign established non-violent resistance as the ANC\'s primary strategy — a position it would maintain until 1961.'),
  fb(4, 'The Defiance Campaign of 1952 was the first large-scale campaign of civil ___ against apartheid. ___ was appointed as the volunteer-in-chief of the campaign.',
    ['disobedience', 'Nelson Mandela'],
    'The Defiance Campaign involved deliberately breaking unjust apartheid laws and accepting arrest. Nelson Mandela played a key leadership role, travelling the country to organise volunteers. The campaign dramatically increased ANC membership and international awareness.'),
  t(5, '## The Freedom Charter (1955) and the Treason Trial\n\n### The Freedom Charter\n\nOn **25-26 June 1955**, approximately 3 000 delegates gathered at the **Congress of the People** in **Kliptown, Soweto** to adopt the **Freedom Charter**.\n\nThe Charter was the product of a broad alliance called the **Congress Alliance**:\n- African National Congress (ANC)\n- South African Indian Congress (SAIC)\n- South African Coloured People\'s Organisation (SACPO)\n- Congress of Democrats (COD) — white allies\n- South African Congress of Trade Unions (SACTU)\n\n### Key Demands\n\n1. "The People Shall Govern" — universal suffrage\n2. "All National Groups Shall Have Equal Rights" — non-racialism\n3. "The People Shall Share in the Country\'s Wealth" — nationalisation of key industries\n4. "The Land Shall Be Shared Among Those Who Work It" — land reform\n5. "All Shall Be Equal Before the Law" — justice\n\n### The Treason Trial (1956-1961)\n\n- In December 1956, **156 anti-apartheid leaders** (including Mandela, Sisulu, Tambo, Luthuli) were arrested and charged with **high treason**\n- The government claimed the Freedom Charter was a communist document aimed at overthrowing the state\n- The trial lasted **five years** — all accused were eventually **acquitted** in March 1961\n- The trial became a platform for anti-apartheid ideas and attracted international attention'),
  q(6, 'What was the outcome of the Treason Trial (1956-1961)?',
    ['All 156 accused were acquitted after a five-year trial', 'All accused were found guilty and imprisoned', 'The trial was cancelled by the government', 'Only Nelson Mandela was found guilty'], 0,
    'After five years, all the accused were acquitted. The court found that the Freedom Charter was not a communist document and did not advocate violent overthrow of the state. However, the trial disrupted the anti-apartheid movement and drained its resources.'),
  t(7, '## Women\'s Resistance: The Women\'s March (1956)\n\nWomen played a crucial role in the struggle against apartheid. The most famous act of women\'s resistance was the **Women\'s March to the Union Buildings** on **9 August 1956**.\n\n### Background\n\n- The government planned to extend **pass laws** to women — previously, only men had been required to carry passes\n- The **Federation of South African Women (FEDSAW)** organised nationwide resistance\n\n### The March\n\n- On 9 August 1956, an estimated **20 000 women** of all races marched to the **Union Buildings** in Pretoria\n- Led by **Lillian Ngoyi**, **Helen Joseph**, **Rahima Moosa**, and **Sophia Williams-De Bruyn**\n- They delivered petitions with over 100 000 signatures to the Prime Minister\'s office (he was absent)\n- The women stood in silence for 30 minutes, then sang: ***"Wathint\' abafazi, wathint\' imbokodo"*** — "You strike a woman, you strike a rock"\n\n### Significance\n\n- Demonstrated the power and determination of women in the liberation struggle\n- **9 August** is now celebrated as **National Women\'s Day** in South Africa\n- The phrase "you strike a rock" became a symbol of women\'s strength and resilience\n\n### Brief Biographies\n\n- **Lillian Ngoyi**: ANC Women\'s League president; first woman on the ANC executive; spent years under house arrest\n- **Helen Joseph**: Anti-apartheid activist and social worker; first person charged under the Suppression of Communism Act; also lived under house arrest for years'),
  fb(8, 'On 9 August 1956, approximately ___ women marched to the Union Buildings in Pretoria. The famous song sung by the marchers was "Wathint\' abafazi, wathint\' ___" — "You strike a woman, you strike a rock."',
    ['20 000', 'imbokodo'],
    'The Women\'s March was one of the largest demonstrations in South African history. The phrase "wathint\' imbokodo" (you strike a rock) symbolised the immovable strength of women in the liberation struggle. 9 August is now National Women\'s Day.'),
  t(9, '## The Sharpeville Massacre (21 March 1960)\n\nThe **Sharpeville Massacre** was a turning point in South African history that changed the nature of resistance to apartheid.\n\n### Background: The PAC and Pass Laws\n\n- In 1959, the **Pan Africanist Congress (PAC)** broke away from the ANC under **Robert Sobukwe**\n- The PAC believed in African nationalism (Africa for Africans) rather than the ANC\'s multi-racial approach\n- On 21 March 1960, the PAC launched a campaign against **pass laws** — calling on people to leave their passes at home and present themselves for arrest at police stations\n\n### What Happened at Sharpeville\n\n- A large, peaceful crowd of approximately **5 000-7 000 people** gathered outside the police station in Sharpeville, near Vereeniging\n- Without warning, police opened fire on the crowd\n- **69 people were killed** and **180 were wounded**\n- Most were shot in the **back** — proving they were running away, not attacking\n- The youngest victim was 13 years old\n\n### Consequences\n\n| Immediate | Long-term |\n|-----------|-----------|\n| Worldwide shock and condemnation | ANC and PAC were **banned** |\n| State of Emergency declared | Both organisations went **underground** |\n| UN Security Council condemned South Africa | Shift from non-violent to **armed resistance** |\n| Mass protests and work stayaways | ANC formed **Umkhonto we Sizwe** (MK) in 1961 |\n| South Africa left the Commonwealth (1961) | 21 March became **Human Rights Day** |'),
  q(10, 'Why was the Sharpeville Massacre a turning point in South African history?',
    ['It led to the banning of the ANC and PAC, and the shift from non-violent to armed resistance', 'It was the first protest against apartheid', 'It led to immediate democratic elections', 'The government immediately repealed all apartheid laws'], 0,
    'After Sharpeville, the government banned the ANC and PAC, eliminating legal channels for protest. This convinced many that non-violent resistance alone could not defeat apartheid. In 1961, the ANC formed Umkhonto we Sizwe (MK), its armed wing, led by Nelson Mandela.'),
];

// --- Lesson 2: The 1960 Formation of the PAC and Sharpeville to Rivonia ---
blockNum = 0;
const ch6_lesson2 = [
  t(1, '## The Formation of the PAC (1959)\n\nThe **Pan Africanist Congress (PAC)** was formed on **6 April 1959** by members who broke away from the ANC. They disagreed with the ANC\'s multi-racial approach and alliance with white, Indian, and Coloured organisations.\n\n### Key Differences Between ANC and PAC\n\n| Feature | ANC | PAC |\n|---------|-----|-----|\n| **Leadership** | Multi-racial alliance | Africanist — led by and for Africans |\n| **Ideology** | Non-racialism; Freedom Charter | Africa for Africans; Pan-Africanism |\n| **Alliances** | Congress Alliance (SAIC, COD, SACPO) | Rejected alliances with non-African organisations |\n| **Approach** | Gradual, strategic resistance | More militant; immediate action |\n| **Leader** | Albert Luthuli (president) | **Robert Sobukwe** (president) |\n\n### Robert Sobukwe\n\n- University lecturer and brilliant intellectual\n- Argued that the Freedom Charter was too influenced by white communists\n- Believed Africans must lead their own liberation\n- After the anti-pass campaign in 1960, he was imprisoned on **Robben Island** under a special law (the "Sobukwe clause") that kept him detained indefinitely\n- Released in 1969 but placed under house arrest in Kimberley until his death in 1978'),
  q(2, 'What was the main ideological difference between the ANC and the PAC?',
    ['The PAC advocated for African-led liberation and rejected multi-racial alliances, while the ANC favoured non-racialism', 'The PAC supported apartheid while the ANC opposed it', 'The ANC supported violence while the PAC was non-violent', 'They had identical ideologies'], 0,
    'The PAC, led by Robert Sobukwe, believed that Africans must lead their own liberation struggle without alliances with white, Indian, or Coloured organisations. The ANC, following the Freedom Charter, embraced non-racialism and worked with organisations of all races through the Congress Alliance.'),
  t(3, '## The Turn to Armed Resistance\n\nAfter the banning of the ANC and PAC in April 1960, the liberation movements made a historic decision to turn to armed struggle.\n\n### Umkhonto we Sizwe (MK) — "Spear of the Nation"\n\n- Founded on **16 December 1961** (the date was chosen symbolically — it was the Day of the Vow/Day of Reconciliation)\n- **Nelson Mandela** was the first commander of MK\n- MK carried out **sabotage attacks** on government buildings, power lines, railway lines, and other infrastructure\n- The strategy was to avoid killing people and to target symbols of apartheid\n- Mandela travelled across Africa and to London to seek training and support\n\n### Poqo — the PAC\'s Armed Wing\n\n- **Poqo** (meaning "pure" or "standing alone") was the PAC\'s armed wing\n- More willing to use violence against people, including attacks on police informers and white civilians\n- Less organised than MK; many members were arrested\n\n### Mandela\'s Famous Statement\n\nIn explaining the decision to take up arms, Mandela later said: *"It was only when all else had failed, when all channels of peaceful protest had been barred to us, that the decision was made to embark on violent forms of political struggle."*'),
  fb(4, 'The ANC\'s armed wing, Umkhonto we Sizwe (MK), was founded on 16 December ___. Its first commander was ___.',
    ['1961', 'Nelson Mandela'],
    'MK was founded on 16 December 1961, a date with symbolic significance in South African history. Mandela led its operations, which initially focused on sabotage of government infrastructure rather than attacks on people.'),
  t(5, '## The Rivonia Trial (1963-1964)\n\nThe **Rivonia Trial** is one of the most important trials in South African — and world — history.\n\n### The Arrest\n\n- On **11 July 1963**, South African police raided **Liliesleaf Farm** in Rivonia, Johannesburg\n- They arrested most of the MK high command, including **Walter Sisulu**, **Govan Mbeki**, **Raymond Mhlaba**, **Ahmed Kathrada**, and others\n- Documents outlining plans for guerrilla warfare (**Operation Mayibuye**) were seized\n- **Nelson Mandela** was already in prison (arrested in August 1962)\n\n### The Trial\n\n- Ten leaders were charged with **sabotage and conspiracy to overthrow the state** — charges that carried the death penalty\n- The trial began in October 1963 at the Palace of Justice in Pretoria\n- The state prosecutor sought the death penalty\n\n### Mandela\'s Speech from the Dock\n\nOn **20 April 1964**, Mandela delivered his famous speech, ending with:\n\n*"During my lifetime I have dedicated myself to this struggle of the African people. I have fought against white domination, and I have fought against black domination. I have cherished the ideal of a democratic and free society in which all persons live together in harmony and with equal opportunities. It is an ideal which I hope to live for and to achieve. But if needs be, it is an ideal for which I am prepared to die."*'),
  q(6, 'What was the significance of Mandela\'s "speech from the dock" at the Rivonia Trial?',
    ['He expressed his commitment to a democratic, non-racial South Africa and his willingness to die for it', 'He admitted guilt and asked for forgiveness', 'He called for immediate armed revolution', 'He denounced the ANC and its policies'], 0,
    'Mandela\'s speech was a powerful statement of principle that turned the trial into a moral indictment of apartheid. Rather than defending himself, he used the dock as a platform to explain why the ANC had turned to armed struggle and to articulate his vision for a democratic South Africa.'),
  t(7, '### The Verdict and Aftermath\n\n- On **12 June 1964**, eight of the accused were found **guilty** and sentenced to **life imprisonment**\n- They narrowly escaped the death penalty, partly due to **international pressure**\n- Mandela, Sisulu, Mbeki, Kathrada, Mhlaba, Mlangeni, Motsoaledi, and Goldberg were sent to **Robben Island** (Goldberg to Pretoria Central as a white prisoner)\n\n### Life on Robben Island\n\n- Prisoners were subjected to hard labour in the lime quarry\n- Black prisoners received worse food, clothing, and conditions than other racial groups\n- Limited visits and letters (one 500-word letter every six months initially)\n- Despite the harsh conditions, prisoners educated each other — Robben Island became known as "the University"\n- Mandela continued to lead the ANC from prison, becoming a global symbol of resistance\n\n### International Reaction\n\n- The Rivonia Trial drew worldwide attention to apartheid\n- The UN called for the release of political prisoners\n- The **Free Mandela** campaign became a global movement\n- South Africa became increasingly isolated internationally\n- Countries began imposing **sanctions** (economic penalties) on South Africa\n\n### The 1960s After Rivonia\n\nWith its leadership imprisoned or in exile, the internal resistance movement was severely weakened during the 1960s. The apartheid government believed it had crushed opposition. But the spirit of resistance would re-emerge powerfully in the **1976 Soweto Uprising**.'),
  fb(8, 'At the Rivonia Trial, the accused were sentenced to ___ imprisonment and sent to ___ Island.',
    ['life', 'Robben'],
    'The Rivonia accused were sentenced to life imprisonment on 12 June 1964. Robben Island, off the coast of Cape Town, became South Africa\'s most notorious political prison. Mandela would spend 18 of his 27 years of imprisonment there.'),
  q(9, 'Why is the Rivonia Trial considered one of the most important trials in South African history?',
    ['It drew worldwide attention to apartheid, made Mandela a global symbol, and led to increasing international isolation of South Africa', 'It resulted in the immediate end of apartheid', 'It was the first trial in South Africa', 'All the accused were acquitted'], 0,
    'The Rivonia Trial transformed Mandela from a local leader into a global symbol of the struggle against racial oppression. The trial drew international attention to the injustice of apartheid and led to growing sanctions, diplomatic isolation, and the Free Mandela campaign.'),
  t(10, '## The Significance of the 1960 Turning Point\n\nThe year 1960 marked a fundamental shift in South African history:\n\n### Before 1960\n\n- Non-violent resistance: petitions, boycotts, civil disobedience\n- Legal political organisations\n- Hope that moral persuasion could change the system\n\n### After 1960\n\n- Violent resistance: sabotage and armed struggle\n- Underground, banned organisations\n- Exile movements in neighbouring countries and London\n- International isolation and sanctions against South Africa\n\n### Timeline of Key Events (1948-1964)\n\n| Year | Event |\n|------|-------|\n| **1948** | National Party wins election; apartheid begins |\n| **1950** | Key apartheid laws passed (Population Registration, Group Areas, Suppression of Communism) |\n| **1952** | Defiance Campaign |\n| **1955** | Freedom Charter adopted at Kliptown |\n| **1956** | Women\'s March; Treason Trial begins |\n| **1959** | PAC formed |\n| **1960** | Sharpeville Massacre; ANC and PAC banned |\n| **1961** | MK formed; South Africa becomes a republic |\n| **1963** | Rivonia arrests |\n| **1964** | Rivonia Trial: life sentences |\n\nThe events of 1960 showed that the apartheid government would use lethal force against peaceful protesters and ban all opposition. This left the liberation movements with no legal options and pushed them towards armed resistance.'),
];

