// --- Lesson 1: Natural Resources and Sustainable Use ---
blockNum = 0;
const ch7_lesson1 = [
  t(1, '## What Are Natural Resources?\n\n**Natural resources** are materials or substances that occur in nature and are used by people to meet their needs.\n\n### Types of Natural Resources\n\n| Type | Definition | Examples |\n|------|------------|----------|\n| **Renewable** | Can be replaced or regenerated naturally within a human lifetime | Solar energy, wind, water, forests, fish |\n| **Non-renewable** | Exist in limited quantities and cannot be replaced once used up | Coal, oil, natural gas, minerals (gold, platinum, iron) |\n| **Flow resources** | Always available, cannot be used up | Solar radiation, wind, tidal energy |\n\n### South Africa\'s Key Natural Resources\n\nSouth Africa is rich in natural resources:\n\n| Resource | Significance |\n|----------|-------------|\n| **Minerals** | World\'s largest reserves of platinum, manganese, chromium; major gold, diamond, iron, and coal producer |\n| **Coal** | Provides over 80% of South Africa\'s electricity; major export |\n| **Water** | Scarce — South Africa is a water-stressed country |\n| **Agricultural land** | Diverse climates support varied farming; only about 12% of land is arable |\n| **Biodiversity** | One of the world\'s 17 megadiverse countries |\n| **Sunshine** | Excellent solar energy potential — one of the highest solar radiation levels globally |\n| **Coastline** | 3 000 km of coastline with marine resources |'),
  q(2, 'What is the difference between renewable and non-renewable resources?',
    ['Renewable resources can be regenerated naturally within a human lifetime, while non-renewable resources cannot', 'Renewable resources are more valuable than non-renewable resources', 'Non-renewable resources come from plants and renewable resources come from underground', 'There is no real difference between them'], 0,
    'Renewable resources like forests, fish, and water can replenish themselves if managed sustainably. Non-renewable resources like coal, oil, and minerals took millions of years to form and cannot be replaced once extracted. This distinction is critical for planning sustainable development.'),
  t(3, '## Unwise Use of Resources\n\nWhen natural resources are used unsustainably, the consequences can be severe and long-lasting.\n\n### Over-Fishing the Oceans\n\nThe world\'s oceans are being over-fished at alarming rates:\n\n- **Over-fishing** means catching fish faster than they can reproduce\n- **70%** of the world\'s fish stocks are fully exploited, over-exploited, or depleted\n- Industrial fishing fleets use advanced technology (sonar, GPS, factory ships) to locate and catch fish\n- Bottom trawling destroys ocean floor habitats\n- By-catch (unwanted species caught accidentally) kills millions of sea creatures\n\n### Over-Fishing in South Africa\n\n- The West Coast rock lobster population has declined by over 90% due to over-harvesting\n- Abalone (perlemoen) poaching is a major problem — organised criminal syndicates export illegally to Asia\n- Hake stocks have been carefully managed through quota systems, but illegal fishing remains a challenge\n- The Marine Living Resources Act (1998) regulates fishing in South African waters\n\n### The Unwise Use of Fossil Fuels\n\n- South Africa depends heavily on **coal** for electricity (over 80%)\n- Burning fossil fuels produces **carbon dioxide (CO₂)**, a greenhouse gas that causes climate change\n- South Africa is the **14th largest emitter** of CO₂ in the world\n- Air pollution from coal-fired power stations causes respiratory diseases, particularly in communities near Mpumalanga\'s coal belt'),
  fb(4, 'Over-fishing means catching fish faster than they can ___. South Africa depends heavily on ___ for over 80% of its electricity generation.',
    ['reproduce', 'coal'],
    'Sustainable fishing means only catching as many fish as can naturally reproduce. South Africa\'s heavy dependence on coal makes it one of the world\'s largest carbon emitters, contributing to climate change while also causing local air pollution and health problems.'),
  t(5, '## Sustainable Use of Resources\n\nSustainable use means using resources at a rate that allows them to be maintained for future generations.\n\n### Sustainable Fishing\n\n- **Catch quotas**: Limiting the amount of fish that can be caught each season\n- **Marine Protected Areas (MPAs)**: Sections of ocean where fishing is restricted or banned, allowing fish stocks to recover\n- South Africa has established several MPAs, including the **iSimangaliso Wetland Park** (a UNESCO World Heritage Site)\n- **Aquaculture** (fish farming): Growing fish, shellfish, and seaweed reduces pressure on wild stocks\n\n### Sustainable Forestry\n\n- **FSC certification**: The Forest Stewardship Council certifies timber from sustainably managed forests\n- **Replanting**: For every tree cut down, new trees must be planted\n- In South Africa, commercial plantations (mostly pine and eucalyptus) are managed sustainably, but they use large amounts of water\n- Indigenous forests (like Knysna forest) are strictly protected\n\n### Reducing Carbon Emissions\n\n- Transitioning from coal to **renewable energy** (solar, wind)\n- South Africa\'s **Renewable Energy Independent Power Producer Procurement Programme (REIPPPP)** has attracted over R200 billion in investment\n- Improving energy efficiency in buildings, transport, and industry\n- **Carbon tax**: South Africa introduced a carbon tax in 2019 to incentivise emission reductions'),
  q(6, 'What is the purpose of Marine Protected Areas (MPAs)?',
    ['To restrict or ban fishing in certain ocean areas, allowing fish stocks to recover', 'To attract tourists to the coast', 'To allow unlimited fishing in specific areas', 'To test new fishing equipment'], 0,
    'MPAs are sections of ocean where fishing and other extractive activities are limited or prohibited. By giving marine ecosystems space to recover, MPAs help rebuild fish stocks, protect biodiversity, and support sustainable fishing in surrounding waters.'),
  t(7, '## The Individual\'s Role in Resource Conservation\n\nEvery person can contribute to sustainable resource use through daily choices.\n\n### Ways Individuals Can Help\n\n| Action | Impact |\n|--------|--------|\n| **Reduce** | Use less water, electricity, and materials; buy only what you need |\n| **Reuse** | Repair items instead of replacing them; use reusable bags and bottles |\n| **Recycle** | Separate waste for recycling — paper, glass, plastic, metal |\n| **Save water** | Fix leaks, take shorter showers, use grey water for gardens |\n| **Save energy** | Switch off lights, use energy-efficient appliances, use solar geysers |\n| **Choose sustainably** | Buy local products, choose sustainable seafood, support eco-friendly businesses |\n| **Plant trees** | Trees absorb CO₂, prevent erosion, and provide habitat |\n| **Reduce food waste** | Plan meals, compost food scraps |\n\n### South Africa\'s Water Crisis\n\n- South Africa is the **30th driest country** in the world\n- Average rainfall is 450 mm per year (world average is 860 mm)\n- Water demand is expected to exceed supply by 2025-2030\n- The **Day Zero** water crisis in Cape Town (2017-2018) showed how close cities can come to running out of water\n- Solutions: water recycling, desalination, rainwater harvesting, fixing leaking infrastructure (municipalities lose 37% of water through leaks)'),
  fb(8, 'The three Rs of waste management are Reduce, ___, and Recycle. South Africa\'s average annual rainfall of ___ mm is well below the world average.',
    ['Reuse', '450'],
    'Reduce, Reuse, Recycle is the hierarchy of waste management — reducing consumption is the most effective strategy, followed by reusing items, and finally recycling. South Africa\'s low rainfall makes water conservation essential for the country\'s future.'),
  q(9, 'What was the "Day Zero" crisis in Cape Town?',
    ['The city nearly ran out of water in 2017-2018 due to severe drought and high demand', 'The city experienced a total power blackout', 'A major earthquake struck the city', 'The city ran out of food supplies'], 0,
    'In 2017-2018, Cape Town came within weeks of becoming the first major city to run out of water. The crisis was caused by a multi-year drought combined with growing demand. Citizens reduced water use to 50 litres per person per day. It was a wake-up call about water scarcity in South Africa.'),
  t(10, '## The Carbon Footprint and Climate Change\n\nA **carbon footprint** is the total amount of greenhouse gases (mainly CO₂) produced by a person, organisation, or country.\n\n### South Africa\'s Carbon Footprint\n\n- South Africa is responsible for about **1.2%** of global CO₂ emissions\n- This is disproportionately high for a country with 0.7% of the world\'s population\n- The main sources: electricity generation (coal), industry, transport\n- Per capita emissions: approximately 7.5 tonnes per year (world average: about 4.5 tonnes)\n\n### Climate Change Impacts on South Africa\n\n| Impact | Detail |\n|--------|--------|\n| **Temperature** | Average temperatures have increased by 1.5°C since 1931 — double the global average |\n| **Rainfall** | Western regions becoming drier; eastern regions may see more intense storms |\n| **Droughts** | More frequent and severe droughts threaten agriculture and water supply |\n| **Sea level rise** | Threatens coastal cities (Cape Town, Durban, Port Elizabeth) |\n| **Biodiversity** | Fynbos and other unique ecosystems threatened by changing climate |\n| **Health** | Heat stress, spread of tropical diseases, malnutrition from crop failure |\n\n### What South Africa Can Do\n\n- Transition from coal to renewable energy\n- Improve public transport to reduce car emissions\n- Protect and restore natural carbon sinks (forests, wetlands, grasslands)\n- Invest in climate adaptation (drought-resistant crops, flood defences)\n- Support international climate agreements (Paris Agreement, 2015)'),
];

// --- Lesson 2: Food Resources and Food Security ---
blockNum = 0;
const ch7_lesson2 = [
  t(1, '## Food Security\n\nA country or household is **food secure** when all people, at all times, have access to sufficient, safe, and nutritious food to maintain a healthy and active life.\n\n### The Four Pillars of Food Security\n\n| Pillar | Meaning |\n|--------|---------|\n| **Availability** | Enough food is produced or imported |\n| **Access** | People can afford to buy food or have land to grow it |\n| **Utilisation** | People have the knowledge and resources to prepare nutritious meals; clean water and sanitation |\n| **Stability** | Food supply is reliable and not disrupted by shocks (drought, conflict, price spikes) |\n\n### Food Insecurity in South Africa\n\n- Despite being a net food exporter, South Africa has widespread food insecurity\n- Approximately **11 million people** (about 20%) experience hunger regularly\n- Food insecurity is highest in rural areas, informal settlements, and among female-headed households\n- Key causes: poverty, unemployment, inequality, and the rising cost of food\n- The COVID-19 pandemic worsened food insecurity, especially for children\n\n### The Paradox\n\nSouth Africa produces enough food to feed its population, yet millions go hungry. This is a problem of **access and distribution**, not production — poverty prevents people from buying the food that is available.'),
  q(2, 'Why does South Africa have widespread hunger despite being a net food exporter?',
    ['Because poverty prevents many people from buying the food that is available — it is a problem of access, not production', 'Because South Africa does not produce enough food', 'Because all food is exported', 'Because South Africans do not eat enough food'], 0,
    'South Africa grows and exports more food than it imports, but extreme inequality means millions of people cannot afford to buy nutritious food. This is a problem of access and distribution, not supply. Addressing food insecurity requires tackling poverty and inequality.'),
  t(3, '## Food Production Methods\n\n### Subsistence Farming vs Commercial Farming\n\n| Feature | Subsistence Farming | Commercial Farming |\n|---------|--------------------|-----------------------|\n| **Purpose** | To feed the farmer\'s family | To sell produce for profit |\n| **Scale** | Small plots | Large farms |\n| **Technology** | Hand tools, animal power | Machinery, irrigation, fertilisers |\n| **Labour** | Family members | Hired workers |\n| **Output** | Low | High |\n| **Examples** | Rural homestead farming in the Eastern Cape | Grain farms in the Free State; vineyards in the Western Cape |\n\n### The Role of Science and Technology\n\nModern agriculture uses science and technology to increase food production:\n\n- **Irrigation**: Bringing water to dry areas; South Africa\'s major irrigation schemes include the Orange River and Breede River projects\n- **Fertilisers and pesticides**: Chemical inputs increase yields but can harm the environment\n- **Mechanisation**: Tractors, combine harvesters, and other machines replace manual labour\n- **Improved seed varieties**: Bred for higher yields, disease resistance, and drought tolerance\n- **Precision agriculture**: GPS, drones, and sensors help farmers use water and fertiliser more efficiently'),
  fb(4, 'Farming to feed one\'s own family is called ___ farming, while farming for profit is called ___ farming.',
    ['subsistence', 'commercial'],
    'Subsistence farming produces food for the farmer\'s family and local community, while commercial farming produces crops and livestock for sale in markets. South Africa has a dual agricultural system with both large commercial farms and millions of small subsistence farmers.'),
  t(5, '## Factory Farming and Its Impacts\n\n**Factory farming** (intensive farming) is the mass production of livestock (chickens, pigs, cattle) in confined indoor conditions.\n\n### Characteristics of Factory Farming\n\n- Animals are kept in **high-density** conditions to maximise production\n- Use of **antibiotics** to prevent disease (which can lead to antibiotic-resistant bacteria)\n- Growth hormones to speed up animal growth\n- Controlled environments (lighting, temperature) to maximise production\n- Feed lots: cattle are fed grain in confined spaces rather than grazing on pasture\n\n### Advantages\n\n- Produces large quantities of affordable meat, eggs, and dairy\n- Efficient use of space and resources\n- Consistent supply throughout the year\n\n### Disadvantages\n\n| Issue | Detail |\n|-------|--------|\n| **Animal welfare** | Animals live in cramped, stressful conditions |\n| **Pollution** | Large amounts of animal waste pollute water and air |\n| **Antibiotic resistance** | Overuse of antibiotics creates drug-resistant bacteria |\n| **Climate change** | Livestock farming produces methane, a powerful greenhouse gas |\n| **Water use** | Producing 1 kg of beef requires approximately 15 000 litres of water |\n| **Health concerns** | High-fat, processed meat diets are linked to heart disease and cancer |'),
  q(6, 'Why is the overuse of antibiotics in factory farming a concern?',
    ['It can lead to antibiotic-resistant bacteria that make medicines less effective for humans', 'It makes the meat taste better', 'It reduces the cost of farming', 'It has no negative effects'], 0,
    'When antibiotics are used routinely in farming (not just to treat sick animals), bacteria can develop resistance. These antibiotic-resistant bacteria can transfer to humans through food, water, and the environment, making previously treatable infections dangerous.'),
  t(7, '## Genetic Modification and Food Production\n\n**Genetically modified organisms (GMOs)** are plants or animals whose DNA has been altered using genetic engineering to produce desired traits.\n\n### How GMOs Work\n\n- Scientists identify a gene that produces a useful trait (e.g., pest resistance, drought tolerance)\n- That gene is inserted into the DNA of a crop plant\n- The modified plant then expresses the desired trait\n\n### GMO Crops in South Africa\n\nSouth Africa is one of the largest producers of GMO crops in Africa:\n- **GM maize**: Resistant to certain insects (Bt maize) and herbicides\n- **GM soybeans**: Herbicide-tolerant, making weed control easier\n- **GM cotton**: Insect-resistant\n\n### The GMO Debate\n\n| Arguments FOR | Arguments AGAINST |\n|--------------|-------------------|\n| Higher yields can help feed growing populations | Long-term health effects are not fully understood |\n| Reduced need for pesticides (some GM crops) | Loss of biodiversity as GM crops replace traditional varieties |\n| Drought-tolerant crops are vital for Africa | Small farmers become dependent on expensive GM seed |\n| Can improve nutritional content (e.g., Golden Rice with Vitamin A) | GM genes may spread to wild plants through pollination |\n| South Africa allows GMOs and regulates them | Consumer choice — people should know if food is GM |'),
  fb(8, 'GMO stands for genetically modified ___. South Africa is one of the largest producers of GM crops in ___, including GM maize, soybeans, and cotton.',
    ['organism', 'Africa'],
    'A GMO is an organism whose DNA has been changed using genetic engineering. South Africa has been growing GM crops since 1997, making it a leader in African agricultural biotechnology. The debate about GMOs involves balancing food security benefits against environmental and health concerns.'),
  q(9, 'What is one concern about GM crops for small-scale farmers in developing countries?',
    ['Small farmers may become dependent on expensive GM seed that they must buy every season', 'GM crops always fail in tropical climates', 'GM crops produce less food than traditional varieties', 'GM crops cannot be grown in South Africa'], 0,
    'Many GM crops are patented by large corporations, meaning farmers cannot save and replant seeds — they must buy new seed every season. This creates dependency and increases costs, which can be devastating for small-scale farmers who already operate on thin margins.'),
  t(10, '## Appropriate Technologies and Sustainable Farming\n\n**Appropriate technology** refers to simple, affordable, and sustainable technologies suited to local conditions.\n\n### Appropriate Technologies for Small-Scale Farming\n\n| Technology | How It Helps |\n|------------|-------------|\n| **Drip irrigation** | Delivers water directly to plant roots, reducing water waste by up to 70% |\n| **Rainwater harvesting** | Collecting and storing rainwater for irrigation |\n| **Composting** | Turning organic waste into natural fertiliser |\n| **Companion planting** | Growing compatible plants together to reduce pests naturally |\n| **Solar-powered pumps** | Using solar energy to pump water for irrigation |\n| **Permaculture** | Designing farming systems that mimic natural ecosystems |\n\n### Organic Farming\n\n- No synthetic fertilisers, pesticides, or GMOs\n- Uses natural pest control, crop rotation, and composting\n- Growing in South Africa — organic produce is increasingly available in supermarkets\n- More expensive but potentially better for health and the environment\n\n### Food Security Solutions for South Africa\n\n- Support small-scale farmers with training, seeds, and access to markets\n- Invest in agricultural research suited to South African conditions\n- Reduce food waste (an estimated 10 million tonnes of food is wasted annually in SA)\n- School feeding programmes (the National School Nutrition Programme feeds over 9 million learners daily)\n- Community food gardens in urban and peri-urban areas\n\nAchieving food security requires a combination of modern technology, traditional knowledge, government support, and individual action.'),
];

