// --- Lesson 1: The 1976 Soweto Uprising ---
blockNum = 0;
const ch8_lesson1 = [
  t(1, '## Background: Bantu Education and Its Impact\n\nTo understand the Soweto Uprising of 1976, we must first understand **Bantu Education** — the apartheid government\'s system of deliberately inferior education for black South Africans.\n\n### The Bantu Education Act (1953)\n\n- Designed by **Hendrik Verwoerd**, who said: *"There is no place for the Bantu in the European community above the level of certain forms of labour"*\n- Transferred control of black education from churches and missions to the government\n- The curriculum was designed to prepare black people only for manual labour and subservient roles\n- **Inferior funding**: The government spent 10-15 times more per white student than per black student\n- **Overcrowded classrooms**: Class sizes of 50-60 students were common\n- **Under-qualified teachers**: Many teachers had only basic education themselves\n- **Limited subjects**: Science, mathematics, and technical subjects were restricted\n\n### The Impact\n\n| Feature | White Schools | Black Schools |\n|---------|--------------|----------------|\n| **Funding per pupil** | R644 (1975) | R42 (1975) |\n| **Student-teacher ratio** | 1:18 | 1:58 |\n| **Compulsory education** | Yes | No |\n| **Facilities** | Libraries, labs, sports fields | Often no basic facilities |\n| **Language of instruction** | English or Afrikaans | Mother tongue (primary), then English/Afrikaans |'),
  q(2, 'What was the purpose of Bantu Education according to Verwoerd?',
    ['To prepare black South Africans only for manual labour and subservient roles in society', 'To provide equal education for all races', 'To encourage black students to pursue university education', 'To teach black students about their cultural heritage'], 0,
    'Verwoerd explicitly stated that Bantu Education would not prepare black people for positions above "certain forms of labour" in white society. It was a system designed to maintain white supremacy by denying quality education to the majority of South Africans.'),
  t(3, '## The Afrikaans Medium Decree\n\nIn 1974, the Department of Bantu Education issued a directive requiring that **50% of subjects** in black secondary schools be taught in **Afrikaans** (with the other 50% in English).\n\n### Why This Was So Offensive\n\n1. **Afrikaans was seen as "the language of the oppressor"** — the language of the apartheid government, police, and pass offices\n2. Most black students and teachers had **little knowledge of Afrikaans** — they would fail subjects taught in a language they barely understood\n3. The decree was imposed **without consultation** — students and teachers had no say\n4. It came on top of decades of inferior education under Bantu Education\n5. English was already the preferred second language — it was seen as useful for international communication and employment\n\n### Student Resistance\n\n- Students formed the **Soweto Students\' Representative Council (SSRC)** to organise resistance\n- **Tsietsi Mashinini** was elected chairman\n- Students planned a peaceful march for **16 June 1976** to protest the Afrikaans decree\n- They were inspired by the **Black Consciousness Movement** led by **Steve Biko**, which emphasised black pride, self-reliance, and psychological liberation'),
  fb(4, 'The ___ Medium Decree required that 50% of subjects in black secondary schools be taught in Afrikaans. The student organisation that organised the protest was the Soweto Students\' Representative Council, led by Tsietsi ___.',
    ['Afrikaans', 'Mashinini'],
    'The Afrikaans Medium Decree was the immediate trigger for the Soweto Uprising. Tsietsi Mashinini, a charismatic student leader, was elected chairman of the SSRC and helped organise the protest march that changed South African history.'),
  t(5, '## 16 June 1976: The Soweto Uprising\n\n### What Happened\n\n- On the morning of **16 June 1976**, an estimated **15 000-20 000 students** from schools across Soweto began a peaceful march towards Orlando Stadium\n- Students carried placards reading **"Down with Afrikaans"** and **"We are not Boers"**\n- Police confronted the march and, after using tear gas, opened fire with live ammunition\n- **Hector Pieterson**, a 12-year-old student, was among the first to be killed\n- The iconic photograph of Hector Pieterson being carried by **Mbuyisa Makhubu**, with his sister **Antoinette Sithole** running alongside, became one of the most famous images in South African history\n\n### The Uprising Spreads\n\n- The violence sparked protests, riots, and school boycotts across the country\n- The uprising spread to Cape Town, Durban, Port Elizabeth, and many other cities and towns\n- Students targeted symbols of apartheid: government buildings, Bantu Administration Board offices, beer halls, and vehicles\n- The government responded with overwhelming force: police and the military used live ammunition, armoured vehicles, and helicopters\n\n### The Toll\n\n- Official government figures: **176 dead** and over 1 000 wounded\n- The actual death toll is believed to be much higher — estimates range from **500 to over 700** killed\n- Thousands of students were arrested, detained, and tortured\n- Many students fled South Africa to join the ANC and PAC in exile, swelling the ranks of the liberation movements'),
  q(6, 'Who was Hector Pieterson?',
    ['A 12-year-old student killed by police during the Soweto Uprising, whose photograph became an iconic image of the struggle', 'A police officer who opened fire on students', 'A government official who ordered the Afrikaans decree', 'A teacher who organised the student march'], 0,
    'Hector Pieterson was a 12-year-old student killed by police bullets on 16 June 1976. The photograph of his body being carried by Mbuyisa Makhubu became one of the most powerful images of the anti-apartheid struggle, symbolising the innocence of the victims and the brutality of the regime.'),
  t(7, '## Steve Biko and the Black Consciousness Movement\n\nThe intellectual foundation for the Soweto generation was the **Black Consciousness Movement (BCM)**, led by **Steve Biko** (1946-1977).\n\n### Key Ideas of Black Consciousness\n\n1. **Psychological liberation**: Black people must free themselves from feelings of inferiority imposed by white supremacy\n2. **Black pride**: Reject negative stereotypes; embrace black identity, culture, and history\n3. **Self-reliance**: Black people must lead their own liberation — not depend on white liberals\n4. **"Black" as political identity**: Included all people oppressed by apartheid — African, Coloured, and Indian\n5. ***"The most potent weapon in the hands of the oppressor is the mind of the oppressed"*** — Biko\'s most famous statement\n\n### Biko\'s Contribution\n\n- Founded the **South African Students\' Organisation (SASO)** in 1969\n- Helped establish community programmes (health clinics, literacy projects)\n- Wrote extensively about the psychology of oppression\n- Was repeatedly **banned** (restricted in movement, association, and publication) by the apartheid government\n\n### Biko\'s Death\n\n- On **12 September 1977**, Steve Biko **died in police custody** at the age of 30\n- He had been arrested and brutally beaten during interrogation\n- He was transported 1 100 km from Port Elizabeth to Pretoria, naked and in the back of a Land Rover, and died of brain injuries\n- The police initially claimed he died during a hunger strike\n- His death caused **international outrage** and further isolated the apartheid government\n- The inquest found that "no one was responsible" — a verdict that outraged the world'),
  fb(8, 'Steve ___ was the leader of the Black Consciousness Movement. He died in police custody on 12 September ___.',
    ['Biko', '1977'],
    'Steve Biko\'s death at the age of 30 while in police custody was one of the most shocking events of the apartheid era. His ideas of psychological liberation and black pride inspired the Soweto generation and continue to influence South African thought today.'),
  q(9, 'What was the most famous statement by Steve Biko?',
    ['"The most potent weapon in the hands of the oppressor is the mind of the oppressed"', '"Workers of the world, unite"', '"You strike a woman, you strike a rock"', '"An eye for an eye makes the whole world blind"'], 0,
    'Biko argued that apartheid\'s greatest power was psychological — making black people believe they were inferior. He argued that true liberation must begin in the mind. This idea was revolutionary because it shifted the focus from external barriers to internal consciousness.'),
  t(10, '## Consequences and Legacy of the 1976 Uprising\n\n### Short-Term Consequences\n\n- The Afrikaans decree was withdrawn\n- The government increased spending on black education (though it remained deeply unequal)\n- Thousands of young South Africans left the country to join the liberation movements in exile\n- International condemnation increased; the **United Nations** imposed a mandatory arms embargo on South Africa in 1977\n- The apartheid government banned 18 Black Consciousness organisations in October 1977\n\n### Long-Term Consequences\n\n1. **A new generation of activists**: The "Class of \'76" revitalised the anti-apartheid struggle\n2. **International isolation**: The uprising, Biko\'s death, and continued repression led to growing sanctions and disinvestment\n3. **The end of passivity**: Black South Africans showed they would no longer accept apartheid quietly\n4. **Education as a battleground**: Schools became centres of resistance throughout the 1980s\n5. **The road to 1990**: The uprising set in motion forces that would ultimately lead to the unbanning of the ANC and Mandela\'s release\n\n### Commemoration\n\n- **16 June** is commemorated as **Youth Day** in South Africa\n- The **Hector Pieterson Memorial and Museum** in Soweto honours the victims\n- The uprising is a reminder that young people have the power to change history'),
];

// --- Lesson 2: 1990 — The Release of Mandela and the Transition to Democracy ---
blockNum = 0;
const ch8_lesson2 = [
  t(1, '## The 1980s: Resistance and Repression\n\nThe 1980s were the most turbulent decade in apartheid history, as resistance intensified and the government responded with increasing violence.\n\n### The United Democratic Front (UDF)\n\n- Founded in 1983 as a broad coalition of **over 600 organisations** opposed to apartheid\n- Included trade unions, civic associations, women\'s groups, youth organisations, and church groups\n- Organised boycotts, stayaways, and mass protests\n- Aligned with the ANC and the Freedom Charter\n\n### The Tricameral Parliament (1983)\n\n- P.W. Botha created a new parliament with three chambers:\n  - **House of Assembly**: White (maintained all real power)\n  - **House of Representatives**: Coloured\n  - **House of Delegates**: Indian\n  - **Black South Africans were excluded entirely**\n- The UDF was formed specifically to oppose this system\n- The tricameral parliament was rejected by the majority of Coloured and Indian voters\n\n### States of Emergency (1985 and 1986)\n\n- The government declared **States of Emergency** in 1985 and 1986\n- Police and military were given sweeping powers: detention without trial, censorship, curfews\n- Approximately **30 000 people were detained** without trial\n- Many detainees were tortured\n- Media restrictions prevented reporting on unrest — foreign journalists were expelled'),
  q(2, 'Why was the Tricameral Parliament of 1983 rejected by most opponents of apartheid?',
    ['Because it completely excluded black South Africans and was seen as an attempt to divide opposition', 'Because it gave too much power to black South Africans', 'Because it abolished apartheid', 'Because it was proposed by the ANC'], 0,
    'The Tricameral Parliament gave Coloured and Indian South Africans limited political representation while completely excluding the black majority. It was widely seen as a divide-and-rule strategy — an attempt to co-opt Coloured and Indian communities while maintaining white supremacy.'),
  t(3, '## External Pressure and Internal Crisis\n\nBy the late 1980s, the apartheid government was under pressure from all sides:\n\n### International Pressure\n\n1. **Economic sanctions**: Many countries banned trade with and investment in South Africa\n2. **Disinvestment**: Major international companies (Barclays Bank, General Motors, IBM) pulled out of South Africa\n3. **Cultural and sports boycotts**: South Africa was banned from the Olympics, international cricket, rugby, and other sports\n4. **The "Free Mandela" campaign**: A global movement demanding Mandela\'s release\n5. **The Anti-Apartheid Movement**: Organisations worldwide lobbied governments to act against South Africa\n\n### Internal Crisis\n\n1. **Economic decline**: Sanctions, disinvestment, and capital flight damaged the economy; the rand lost value; inflation and unemployment rose\n2. **Ungovernability**: Mass protests, rent boycotts, and school boycotts made townships ungovernable\n3. **Military stalemate**: The Battle of Cuito Cuanavale (1988) in Angola showed the limits of South Africa\'s military power\n4. **Moral crisis**: Growing numbers of white South Africans opposed apartheid — the **End Conscription Campaign** challenged military service\n5. **The cost of apartheid**: Maintaining separate systems for each race was economically unsustainable'),
  fb(4, 'Major international companies pulled out of South Africa through a process called ___. The Battle of ___ (1988) in Angola showed the limits of South Africa\'s military power.',
    ['disinvestment', 'Cuito Cuanavale'],
    'Disinvestment (companies withdrawing from South Africa) was one of the most effective forms of international pressure against apartheid. The Battle of Cuito Cuanavale was the largest battle in Africa since World War II and demonstrated that the apartheid military could not win its regional wars.'),
  t(5, '## F.W. de Klerk and the Unbanning Speech (2 February 1990)\n\nOn **2 February 1990**, President **F.W. de Klerk** delivered a speech to Parliament that changed the course of South African history.\n\n### Key Announcements\n\n1. The **ANC, PAC, SACP**, and other banned organisations were **unbanned**\n2. Political prisoners who were not guilty of violent crimes would be **released**\n3. The **State of Emergency** restrictions were lifted\n4. The death penalty was suspended\n5. Media restrictions were eased\n6. The way was opened for **negotiations** towards a new South Africa\n\n### Why Did De Klerk Act?\n\n1. **The Cold War had ended**: The collapse of the Soviet Union removed the "communist threat" argument\n2. **Economic crisis**: Sanctions and isolation were crippling the economy\n3. **Internal resistance**: The apartheid system was becoming ungovernable\n4. **Military stalemate**: South Africa could not win its regional wars\n5. **International pressure**: South Africa was a pariah state\n6. **Personal conviction**: De Klerk later said he came to believe apartheid was morally indefensible\n7. **Mandela\'s negotiations**: Secret talks between Mandela and government officials had been ongoing since 1985'),
  q(6, 'What did F.W. de Klerk announce on 2 February 1990?',
    ['The unbanning of the ANC, PAC, and SACP, and the opening of negotiations for a new South Africa', 'The continuation of apartheid with minor reforms', 'The declaration of war on neighbouring countries', 'Free elections for all South Africans'], 0,
    'De Klerk\'s speech on 2 February 1990 was one of the most important moments in South African history. By unbanning the liberation movements and opening the way for negotiations, he acknowledged that apartheid had failed and that a new South Africa had to be negotiated.'),
  t(7, '## The Release of Nelson Mandela (11 February 1990)\n\nNine days after De Klerk\'s speech, on **11 February 1990**, **Nelson Mandela** walked out of **Victor Verster Prison** near Paarl — a free man after **27 years** of imprisonment.\n\n### Mandela\'s Imprisonment (1964-1990)\n\n| Period | Location | Conditions |\n|--------|----------|------------|\n| 1964-1982 | **Robben Island** | Hard labour in lime quarry; small cell; limited contact |\n| 1982-1988 | **Pollsmoor Prison** (Cape Town) | Better conditions; began secret negotiations with government |\n| 1988-1990 | **Victor Verster Prison** (Paarl) | House on prison grounds; meetings with government officials |\n\n### The Release\n\n- Billions of people worldwide watched Mandela\'s release on television\n- He walked out hand-in-hand with his wife **Winnie Mandela**\n- In his first public speech (at the Grand Parade in Cape Town), Mandela:\n  - Called for **peace and reconciliation**\n  - Reaffirmed the ANC\'s commitment to a **non-racial, democratic South Africa**\n  - Acknowledged De Klerk as "a man of integrity"\n  - Called on the international community to **maintain sanctions** until apartheid was fully dismantled\n\n### Mandela\'s Message\n\n*"Our march to freedom is irreversible. We must not allow fear to stand in our way."*\n\nDespite 27 years in prison, Mandela emerged without bitterness. His commitment to reconciliation over revenge would define the transition to democracy.'),
  fb(8, 'Nelson Mandela was released from ___ Prison on 11 February 1990 after ___ years of imprisonment.',
    ['Victor Verster', '27'],
    'Mandela\'s release from Victor Verster Prison was a moment of global significance. After 27 years — first on Robben Island, then at Pollsmoor and Victor Verster — he emerged as a symbol of reconciliation, calling for peace and unity rather than revenge.'),
  t(9, '## Negotiations and the Road to Democracy (1990-1994)\n\nThe period between Mandela\'s release (1990) and the first democratic elections (1994) was difficult and often violent.\n\n### Key Steps\n\n| Date | Event |\n|------|-------|\n| **1990** | ANC, PAC, SACP unbanned; Mandela released; Groote Schuur Minute (agreement to start negotiations) |\n| **1991** | Remaining apartheid laws repealed (Group Areas Act, Land Acts, Population Registration Act); CODESA 1 begins |\n| **1992** | CODESA 2 collapses; **Boipatong massacre** (June); Bisho massacre (September) |\n| **1993** | Multi-party negotiations resume; **Chris Hani** assassinated (April); date for elections set; Interim Constitution agreed; Mandela and De Klerk share **Nobel Peace Prize** |\n| **1994** | **27 April**: First democratic elections — **all South Africans** vote for the first time |\n\n### Violence During the Transition\n\n- An estimated **14 000 people** died in political violence between 1990 and 1994\n- Much of the violence was between ANC and **Inkatha Freedom Party (IFP)** supporters, particularly in KwaZulu-Natal and on the Witwatersrand\n- There was evidence of a government-linked **"third force"** that instigated violence to undermine negotiations\n- The assassination of **Chris Hani** (12 April 1993) nearly derailed the process — Mandela\'s calm response helped prevent an explosion of violence'),
  q(10, 'What nearly derailed the transition to democracy in April 1993?',
    ['The assassination of SACP leader Chris Hani by a right-wing extremist', 'A military coup by the South African Defence Force', 'The outbreak of a new world war', 'The collapse of the South African economy'], 0,
    'Chris Hani, the popular leader of the South African Communist Party and former MK chief of staff, was assassinated on 10 April 1993 by Janusz Walus, a right-wing Polish immigrant. Mandela addressed the nation on television, urging calm and preventing what could have been a catastrophic breakdown.'),
  t(11, '## The 1994 Elections and the Birth of Democracy\n\nOn **27 April 1994**, South Africa held its first **democratic elections** — the first in which all South Africans, regardless of race, could vote.\n\n### The Elections\n\n- Nearly **20 million South Africans** voted over three days (26-28 April)\n- Many waited in queues for hours — some for the entire day\n- Despite fears of violence, the elections were peaceful\n- International observers declared the elections substantially **free and fair**\n\n### Results\n\n| Party | Percentage | Seats |\n|-------|-----------|-------|\n| **ANC** | 62.6% | 252 |\n| **National Party** | 20.4% | 82 |\n| **Inkatha Freedom Party** | 10.5% | 43 |\n| Others | 6.5% | 23 |\n\n### Nelson Mandela: President of South Africa\n\n- On **10 May 1994**, Nelson Mandela was inaugurated as the first **democratically elected president** of South Africa\n- He formed a **Government of National Unity** that included the NP and IFP\n- In his inauguration speech, Mandela declared: *"Never, never and never again shall it be that this beautiful land will again experience the oppression of one by another."*\n\n### The Significance\n\n- The peaceful transition from apartheid to democracy is considered one of the great achievements of the 20th century\n- South Africa avoided the civil war that many had predicted\n- The 1996 Constitution, with its Bill of Rights, is one of the most progressive in the world\n- **27 April** is celebrated as **Freedom Day** — a reminder that democracy is never guaranteed and must be actively protected'),
];

